{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1410223-additional-spirit-broods","id":1410223,"title":"Additional Spirit Broods","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-08-24T22:15:34-07:00","id":1410223,"title":"Additional Spirit Broods","content":"I find spirit broods a useful tool when I run chronicles. They're an easy way to identify spirits friendly to Garou, particularly non-Theurges who lack the many tools Theurges have to navigate the Umbra. That's good for learning Gifts and creating spirit NPCs for them to interact with. It also provides an easy to way to provide some soap opera/roleplay in regards to spirits. I like to liven up the umbra and its spirit inhabitants so it seems dynamic.<br /> <br /> We were given spirit broods for each of the tribal totems in Axis Mundi, but I don't think there's ever been published additional broods. I think that's a shame as I said because I consider them a useful tool. I think there are other spirit broods that are roughly equivalent in power and prestige to the tribal totems. I am interested in hearing from you if you've ever used the concept, and if you've added additional broods in your games.<br /> <br /> The easiest additions would be those spirits who have (or once had) Changing Breeds of their own, and were included as pack totems in the corebooks. So that woud leave us the following Incarna who should have extensive spirit broods.<br /> <br /> Boar<br /> Bear<br /> Raven<br /> Fox<br /> <br /> I think Coyote is complex because officially the actual spirit who is patron of the Nuwisha is not Coyote the animal per se, but the more universal Trickster. So I would say Trickster is the actual Incarna with the brood, and then place all the various &quot;masks&quot; as part of his spirit brood like Coyote (although I dislike that many of the aspects in the Nuwisha breedbook are straight from human mythology because I think those aspects are more High Umbral than what a Gaian spirit would have). Including Fox here would also make sense, but I don't think Trickster should have TWO Changing Breeds.<br /> <br /> I would also include Incarna not listed as Garou totems, but who represent other changing breeds like Lion (and possibly other Bastest breeds), Crocodile, Hyena, Serpent, Bull, Bat, Spider, and Shark.<br /> <br /> If there's enough interest in this topic, we can even brainstorm what other spirits should be part of each brood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-26T14:20:54-07:00","id":1410564,"title":"","content":"Brainstorming spirit broods sounds cool, although I have no brainpower for it right now.<br /> <br /> I think of the Trickster as a Celestine, with various Incarnae in his/its brood, incl. Raven. But I don't know if there's any textual support for that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">She/Her. I am literal-minded and write literally. If I don't say something explicitly, please never assume I implied it. The only exception is if I try to make a joke.</span></span></span></div> <span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">My point of view may be different from yours but is equally valid.</span></span></span><br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1x1ycoxJF0G9niMt5D3Pp7owZ7tw8AJvL?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">Exalted-cWoD-ArM url mega-library</a>. Exalted <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/663334-random-name-generator\" target=\"_blank\">name-generators</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/896-erinys","name":"Erinys","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar896_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4235"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-08-26T21:47:16-07:00","id":1410611,"title":"","content":"I agree it makes sense, but since the game has Corax, Kitsune, and Nuwisha, I'm reluctant to group all of them under a single Trickster Incarna. I think its only Nuwisha that explicitly states their breed totem is actually Trickster in guise of Coyote. Plus in the game, Raven isn't explicitly a Trickster figure although there are some mythologies that put Raven in that spot.<br /> <br /> The game is actually fairly loose with the same spirit being seen as more than one thing, and having the same spirit appear under different spirit broods. Dream Ravens are under Chimera; Hrafn are under Fenris; Rogue Raven Gafflings under Grandfather Thunder; and Raven itself is presumably its own because of the Corax. And there are other similar examples. So there's no problem having similar spirits (given a different name for convenience sake) in different broods. So there can still be trickster Raven spirits for Trickster/Coyote even with Raven itself having its own brood.<br /> <br /> If Trickster is actually some Celestine level figure as you said, then we're back to having the different breeds under its own thing and its own Incarna. But I'm unsure if I can accept Trickster as a Celestine since the game makes a pretty strong match that Celestines match up with celestial/astronomical objects, and don't act as direct totem patrons for the Changing Breeds. Instead, I think I prefer Trickster as a powerful Incarna. Coyote (as its own Incarna, and this is the source of the Pack Totem of the Garou) would be under him specifically because of existing game lore; but the other Incarna might simply be friends with Trickster/Coyote and outside that spirit brood.<br /> <br /> I think there's many potential solutions that individual STs could like. And I don't think we'll come to agreement on a single version. Trickster probably approves of that.<br /> <br /> I think this is a good discussion topic, but hope it doesn't overwhelm the thread as there are many other interesting things under it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-09-04T16:54:25-07:00","id":1411934,"title":"","content":"I am working on a list of possible spirit broods beginning with Boar, as I think he's one of the easier ones to do. The first thing I have to do is determine the appropriate &quot;spiritual correspondence&quot; of the Totem Incarna to determine the type of spirits that belong in the brood. Then I need to assign/create spirits that reflect that. That's taking a while since there are some very creative spirit examples in Axis Mundi so it's not just picking some similar animal spirits and creating a brood.<br /> <br /> The three main correspondences for Boar that I have are 1) dangerous prey animals (these may be small or medium sized omnivores, but aren't primarily predators - they're still typically the prey of larger predators), 2) diggers of the soil (boars often forage on roots, tubers, and such), and 3) cleansers of the land (this is not do to boars so much as the Grondr's supposed role).<br /> <br /> The Axis Mundi book typically only has 4-8 spirits for each Spirit Brood, but these are supplemented by spirit totems in the tribebooks which I consider must reflect other spirits of that totem incarna's brood. Furthermore, besides spirits that are clearly associated with the totem's main purpose, most totems also have other spirits that provide some variety in order to make all kinds of totems available to the tribe (Fenris' spirit brood includes spirits of joy; Unicorns has a war spirit; Stag has spirits to enact his vengeance, etc.) So this requires some deft touch as well as just being creative.<br /> <br /> = = =<br /> Still hoping to hear from people on other potential candidates of sprits who are Totem Incarna with their own spirit broods.<br /> <br /> Just thought of an important one I left out - Turtle. We know that Turtle was once such a spirit since he was a tribal totem and thus had to have a spirit brood. I think canonically Turtle is now in slumber and/or angry at the Garou and thus unavilable as a totem for them. So it brings some interesting questions as to what has happened to his spirit brood, and how they might react to Garou (and other Changing Breeds now). So I think it's a great example of what an ST might do with the spirit brood concept to assist roleplay in the Umbra.<br /> <br /> From a quick look at the Croatan Song book, we have a few quick examples of Turtle's brood. The Earth-burrowers are mentioned as &quot;ancient allies of the Croatan.&quot; Probably the Corn-Maiden and possibly Trout, though neither is explicitly written as connected to the Croatan or Turtle (though they are mentioned in the W20 corebook description). W20 also mentions Thunderbird, but we know that spirit (or perhaps an aspect) also has ties to the Wendigo. Turtle's spiritual correspondences seems to be burrowing animals, shelled animals, and earth spirits; but I would also add amphibious animals, honor, and stamina/fortitude/stability.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-16T00:39:38-08:00","id":1432384,"title":"","content":"As one might tell from my posts on <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1430172-whippoorwill-as-a-totem\">Whippoorwill</a>, I am continuing to look at developing spirit broods for use in my game. I find it useful as a guide for roleplay among spirits, and I am also working on introducing spririt affinity and notoriety in the game, and using broods as a means to distinguish reactions to decisions of the PCs. Offending one spirit may harm one's reputation much more within that spirit's brood than the spirit population in general, and some broods may care less or not at all depending on what was done. Likewise for building rapport and strength.<br /> <br /> So I finally came to some conclusions about a spirit brood around <b>Bear</b>. What are the main correspondences that associate around this Incarna and his brood? Well, I'm saying..<br /> <br /> Bear attracts solitary animals (as opposed to herds, packs, or other extended groups; omnivores in the Order carnivora (as opposed to omnivores among other animals like ungulates); animals and plants that are know for &quot;powerful medicine&quot; or healing; the protective nature of a mother bear defending her cubs; hibernation and rebirth; and immense physical strength. Some of this obviously ties into what's known with the Gurahl, and that's on purpose. What was harder was expanding on that. Another thing I like is that bears are one of the few animals besides man that have plantigrade feet, and they stand and sit much like men do. This often makes bears seem very human to us. They also have a superb sense of smell - much better than a wolf's (a wolf's sense of smell is about 100 times that of man's; a bear's is between 2000-3000 times better).<br /> <br /> So what kind of spirits do we have? I first looked at the canonical pack totems.<br /> <br /> There's Rhino. It's a solitary animal, and obviously strong. At first, I hesitated because bears and rhinos don't share a common environment and I thought that should hurt brood membership. But after some research, I saw the Indian Rhinoceros historically overlapped with certain bear species in India and SE Asia. That was enough for me to add it. It also helps that rhino horn is associated with certain mystical properties. Of course, Unicorn likely has some Rhinos as well. But it's not essentially for all spirits of one type to be exclusive to one totem.<br /> <br /> Weasel is another one. Males tend to have solitary territories, and there are a lot of folklore concerning the magic and power of weasels.<br /> <br /> I like Butterfly as an addition as well. It's often associated with souls which fits well with Bear's rebirth portfolio.<br /> <br /> I thought Salmon also made a good addition. Its another powerful, magical animal, and bears often eat them. Salmon is a spirit that already has ties to other tribes like Wendigo and Fianna, but it's not inappropriate for some to also be associated with Bear.<br /> <br /> There's also Frog and Toad, and these small animals are often thought to be very powerful magically. So it's appropriate for association with Bear. As amphibians, they'd likely also be associated with Turtle. So I'd split the difference and arbitrarily assign one to Turtle and the other to Bear.<br /> <br /> Badger is mentioned in the Red Talon tribebook, but it has a lot of aspects better associated with Bear than Griffin. Its solitary, an omnivore, and lives in dens under the ground. It's not a problem that Red Talons like the totem.<br /> <br /> Then there are animals that have never been given totem write ups. Of these I like skunk and mole as they're solitary animals, but sufficiently different to give Bear some diversity. I've also thought about the European Otter (there are Otter totems in the books, but they all seem to be based on North American ones associated with Wendigo and Children of Gaia). <br /> <br /> Possibly the honey bee as well. I've always thought honey bees (or ants) were much better than Cockroach as being a Weaver totem. And despite the obvious connection to modern day cities with Cockroaches, I would think the early Warders would have done better than going with another insect. However, perhaps we can play on that by saying Honey Bee was not available as a result of it belonging to Bear, and so the early Warders were dissuaded from every trying to develop into a tribe using Bee as a totem.<br /> <br /> Fox too is a solitary animal with lots of magic, and I'd think he'd make a nice addition. But given that Kitsune exist, I am thinking Fox is best served having its own spirit brood.<br /> <br /> And of course, besides simply picking animals, we can develop various aspects of Bear - one as a warrior/protector, another as a healer/shaman, and so on. Just slap some interesting names and distinguish their nature.<br /> <br /> I remember Mangi the Death Bear spirit mentioned in the level five Gift that can bring people back from the dead. But I don't think such a spirit would be part of Bear's brood. It seems more like a particularly powerful aspect of Death - more like an Epiphling, separate from the Brood. Does any remember any other spirits mentioned in the Gurahl breedbook or elsewhere?<br /> <br /> The hard part is always coming up with interesting, unusual spirits like those found in Axis Mundi. I'm still brainstorming those. But probably something based around its amazing sense of smell. And perhaps a spirit known for associations with humans given its morphology and the ancient bear cult of Stone Age people. And likely a spirit of hiberation as well. Perhaps a more whimsical, friendly spirit based on so many beloved characters for children in books, movies, and toys.<br /> <br /> One reason I really like Bear as an Incarna with its own brood is that the Garou see Bear from an unusual perspective. They recognize his power (I have always liked the Bear totem write up; it's a totem of War, but its powers are more directed towards healing), but also don't trust the spirit. The Gurahl are usually depicted as the enemies of the Garou, but the Bear totem isn't necessarily negative towards them. Overall, they seem to view Bear as a treacherous figure and not dependable. It's an interesting dynamic, and it's one I'd like to explore that as part of its general brood as well. One just needs to figure out how to depict its contradictory nature.<br /> <br /> I am thinking perhaps the spirits of this brood are particularly sensitive and keen to overreact. If offended, the brood acts much more hostilely and much quicker than other broods; but if treated well; they can be very generous. And perhaps they don't trust the Garou any more than the Garou trust them, so negotiations and contact by the PCs with these spirits are at a greater difficult and require more successes. Another option is that this brood starts with some affinity/notoriety against other broods. So characters from the usual tribes (Silver Fangs, Get of Fenris, Shadow Lords) are treated more negatively initially, but certain others (I'd limit this - but the Uktena and Wendigo and Silent Striders would be good contenders) are more positive. The rest being neutral. Contacting the spirits of this brood should be more of a high risk/high reward for Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-01-17T13:19:20-08:00","id":1432534,"title":"","content":"One issue with spirit broods is the issue of how to run them when certain tribal totems may not seem to be &quot;present&quot; in the setting. This is mainly an issue in historical chronicles like one in the Dark Age setting, or a pre-Columbian contact North America. The &quot;generic animal&quot; totems seem universal. But the ones based on legendary and mythological creatures would seem to be heavily based on local human culture. Are Wendigo and Uktena present in Europe or Asia? How do Garou in North America (or even the Middle East or East Asia) see &quot;Fenris&quot; if they don't have reference to Norse mythology? Or in fact, how did the Garou see these spirits in the thousands of years before there were human cultures that told stories about Fenris, Pegasus, and the like.<br /> <br /> My solution is that the Garou still knew about these Incarna and their spirit broods, although not to the extent that their tribes and the tribes around them did. They just interpreted them in different ways.<br /> <br /> So in my chronicle's Dark Ages, the Garou Europe (and in Asia) knew that &quot;Uktena&quot; existed and interacted with his brood of serpents and river monsters and exotic animals. But they only knew him as the Horned Serpent, and they knew only general things about the totem by the way his brood interacted with them. He was very mysterious and unknown. &quot;Wendigo&quot; was seen as the spirit of a particular bad winter. The Get of Fenris likely called it Fimbulwinter. The Black Furies might have called him the Boreal Wind. Or a more generic term like Great Winter or Blizzard might be used. They'd be aware that the spirit of winter in the extreme north existed and had a lot of power, but he was a mysterious figure.<br /> <br /> I haven't given as much thought as to how the Pure One tribes would have interpreted corresponding Incarna, but they would have done the same. Fenris is fairly easy to translate over - a giant, monstrous, dangerous wolf. You can call that by a variety of names. Since Fenrir means &quot;fen dwelling&quot;, it's probably some equivalent of that &quot;The Lone Wolf in the Bogs&quot;. The Pure Ones wouldn't know Pegasus as a winged horse, but they did know that there was some kind of protective spirit sent from the moon to watch over women. Griffin was probably a spirt that watched over the parts of nature that were inviolate and not to be disturbed, and was known by a variety of names.<br /> <br /> At least, that is how I run it.<br /> <br /> Of course, now it's a trap that if there is some Incarna with a spirit brood out there, then it means there HAS to be a Garou tribe or Changing Breed behind it. I'm leery of that. I'd like there to be some powerful Incarna with broods that are still independent, just enough to disprove that theory. But I'm not sure where to go for inspiration.<br /> <br /> <b>Old Man Sea</b> (mentioned in the first Players Guide as a pack totem, but then never mentioned again it seems) could be one of them. Historically <b>Mammoth or Elephant</b> might have been its own Incarna with its own spirit brood, and they have since found other Incarna to serve. Maybe we should add an Incarna to represent the animals who live underneath the earth, fossorial creatures. Maybe Earthworm or Shrew. And we should probably have some kind of lizard separate from Crocodile or whatever rules Mokole. <b>Bison/Buffalo</b> could have been one as well. And given the near extinction of the Buffalo, that could have lead to all sorts of interesting adventures in the Wyld West period or even now. Are there Garou who work to keep Bison as one of the Great Incarna? Are members of his brood defecting? What does that mean in the spirit world? It might be interesting to have Bison/Buffalo as an Incarna still today, but incredibly weak and possibly ready to fall from power, or even to the Wyrm. That might make an interesting chronicle hook.<br /> <br /> Something else I like is the <b>Wren</b>, the small passerine bird. In Europe, the wren was known as the &quot;King of the Birds&quot;, and there's a cute story of how the Wren defeated the obvious contenders for that title. While Falcon reigns supreme among Garou, we already know that Owl is its own Incarna not beholden to Falcon. And Raven is likely its own Incarna as well. So another bird as its own Incarna and brood of small, plucky animal spirits should be fine.<br /> <br /> I'm sure there are other good candidates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 01-18-2021, 03:54 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-01T21:43:11-08:00","id":1434224,"title":"","content":"I have been giving more thought on a theoretical Incarna brood leader of spirits that live under the earth. I learned a new word today - bioturbators. These are animals and plants that rework soils by digging, eating, and defecating sediments so that soil is altered and remixed. They serve an important role as ecosystem engineers. Bioturbation is an essential process to increase biological and chemical diversity by transporting nutrients. And I think that's a great hook for a spirit brood (tillers of the soil). It doesn't need to be animals that live underground, but those that rework the soil. Or at least a combination of the two. While there are large and small animals that do this, most of this work is done by small invertebrates. (OK, science class is now over).<br /> <br /> Moles and badgers fit into this category, but per an earlier post I'll likely stick them into Bear's brood for other reasons. But mentioning them for consideration. Or do as other writers are done, and be fine with the &quot;same&quot; spirits being in multiple broods with slightly different names to distinguish them.<br /> <br /> Other good animals include shrews, gophers, prairie dogs, earthworms, and slugs. They're all associated with living underground, recognizable, and very different from each other. Armadillos could also be part of the group.<br /> <br /> In bodies of water, these include various bivalves like clams and oysters; crabs and shrimp; to fish like salmon and even walruses, although these last bits are getting far from creatures that also &quot;live underground&quot;. But clams and oysters should still be good candidates.<br /> <br /> But there are other animals that could do the same as well - scorpions, some spiders, termites, and ants. Usually arachnids and insects aren't included in broods because they are consigned to the role of the Weaver, although the Weaver's actual servants aren't truly spiders and such, but Triatic spirits that take the form of them. But much like the Wyrm corrupts Gaian spirits in its service, it's possible other Gaian spirits might be friendly to Weaver spirits as well. Despite, I think at least some of these animals could be included.<br /> <br /> Obviously, I don't think any of these creatures have any spirit or totem write ups, so we're in completely new territory. And once again, we'd be on our own in coming up with more creative spirits to expand the brood to be more than just a list of animal spirits. However, our &quot;tillers of the soil&quot; concept is strong and I think we can come up stuff associated like this, like Garden Gnomes who watch over and protect the work of people who tend their own gardens, or a protective spirit that watches over farmers. We might also have some kind of architect like spirits that transcend ant and termite colonies to embrace underground constructions. Or cave spirits to represent natural tunnels. And as creatures that live underground, they are associated with the underworld and thus the dead. Besides spirits associated with that, it could make a good home for some of the spookier spirits once affiliated with Lion of the White Howlers such as Cairn Rattler. The W20 White Howler tribebook description of Cairn Rattler (spelled caern rattler, but &quot;cairn&quot; is probably a better spelling for this spirit for obvious reasons) says the spirit disappeared after their fall. But this would be a good place to put it if people wanted to reuse the totem.<br /> <br /> The question is what creature is the best candidate for Incarna level status, a spirit that is formidable enough to take on Thunder, Stag, Unicorn, Rat, and others?<br /> <br /> There's a lot to consider. Which of them have enough spiritual resonance to have accrued enough power that they could dominate or protect other spirits? Ants are probably the strongest such candidate, but they're almost too strong and have way too many connections to other correspondences outside the scope. They definitely have the live underground and move soils part down pat. But they're probably the most Weavery of all animals out there, and much of their cultural considerations involve hard work and cooperation. They'd also be an unlikely candidate to extend protection to spirits of mammals and such. So as much as I like them, I think I need to exile them to some other theoretical spirit brood that the Garou likely do not interact with at all.<br /> <br /> Earthworm is another strong candidate. It fills this spirit roll perfectly as well, and is instantly recognizable. It would also make an interesting addition to our growing &quot;incarna pantheon&quot;. But perhaps too different. Should Earthworm truly be the master of the tillers of soil? Or simply one of its more powerful friends and servants?<br /> <br /> If we want something more traditional, our strongest candidates are mole and shrew. Gophers are found only in the Americas, and I prefer concepts that are more universal around the world so they could gather more spiritual power from being observed. I reluctantly might have to go with mole even though I first identified him as a potential member of Bear's brood. But Shrew is also worthy to consider. Something I did not know until I began reading more is that the Shrew family is likely the most successful family of Mammals in the world in population size. Who would have guessed? It's also not a rodent, which helps distinguish it from Rat. They're also interesting in other ways - as shrews are some of the few mammals that can be venomous, and some species are the only terrestrial mammal that can echolocate. They can also go into torpor. Both moles and shrews also have a lot of folklore about them. I think Mole is the more obvious choice, but Shrew offers a lot of diversity in the kind of spirits and themes that could be associated with, making a more diverse spirit brood. <br /> <br /> \u200bDoes anyone have a favorite?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-02T11:51:41-08:00","id":1434280,"title":"","content":"A lot of fascinating stuff here. Will read again, think about it some, then respond.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26645-pwtucker","name":"pwtucker","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26645_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"68"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-03T16:58:35-08:00","id":1434433,"title":"","content":"I just realized by going with a spirit brood approach, that there's an obvious gap in my system. And that is I need to address the role of <b>Wolf</b>.<br /> <br /> The game has always skirted around the issue of the Wolf Incarna. I've had players ask me if it is possible to have Wolf as a totem. My stock answer is no. Wolf already blesses the Garou, and he doesn't want to treat his children unequally by favoring some as a tribal or pack totem. If there is no totem that can be picked, then for 99% of games out there, Wolf doesn't need to be addressed in the game. There can be wolf spirits, but because most people don't utilize the brood concept extensively, they just need to be generic spirits out there. It doesn't matter if they belong to any brood. In my scheme though, I need to address it.<br /> <br /> Obviously Wolf needs to be a Major Incarna, and have his own spirit brood. But in terms of their relationship with Garou, that brood should be distinguished from other broods. As the spirit parent to all Garou, the brood should be known for their approachability towards other Garou. They should be favorable to all Garou in any kind of affinity/notoriety system. They should willingly speak the Garou Tongue so any auspice can talk to them without need of a special gift like Spirit Speech. How they might treat Ronin or even BSDs is open to some debate and discussion, but obviously Wolf is pro-Gaia and wants all his children to be a single family. We also need to make sure Wolf is distinct from Fenris (which is likely an aspect of Wolf that has achieved prominence, or in some interpretations could be something else). The corebook defaults &quot;wolf spirits&quot; as teachers for a lot of Gifts (breed, auspice, and tribe), so the idea of a common body of spirits available to all Garou fits in well.<br /> <br /> Ironically, I see Wolf as a distant parent, and probably more hard to contact than most Incarna by the Garou. Again, he doesn't want to show favor by allowing one Garou, or one pack, or one tribe to claim they speak for him and thereby gain authority over others. In terms of spirit politics, he also doesn't want to anger any of the tribal Incarna by stepping on their toes. So he is probably Olympian and distant at least in terms of Garou. His brood is enough to be on friendly terms. He himself is even more inaccessible. He and his brood might act differently if one of the other Changing Breeds tries to contact him as he is just another powerful spirit to them, but they don't accord him any special place among their people. But I think this opens some interesting roleplay opportunities here for STs to have NPC Changing Breeds with some tie to the Wolf spirit. Especially for breeds like Corax that should have some connection to such spirits.<br /> <br /> So who should make up Wolf's spirit brood?<br /> <br /> Obviously wolf spirits belong here. I also think wild canids in general are a good fit. Jackals certainly make sense. And if Jackal is part of Wolf's brood, then the Striders being part of the Garou Nation makes a lot more sense even if their Pure Breed form is a jackal. This should also extend to dholes, African Painted Dogs, and even dingoes. For those who like the Red Talons having members that are non-wolves (I am not one of them, but tastes are different), this makes for an elegant solution for why its allowed but others. Similarly, if the Bunyip are Garou per canon (instead of a Changing Breed like I have them in my home chronicles), then Thylacine was also part of Wolf's brood. So despite them not being a wolf or even a placental mammal, the Bunyip are still Garou. A corollary to that logic is that canids with their own Changing Breeds should not be part of the brood - so that leaves out foxes and coyotes.<br /> <br /> Another obvious set of spirits are the totem spirits that seem to be an Aspect of Wolf. There's the Old Wolf of the Woods (Red Talons tribebook) and Winter Wolf (Rage Across Russia). I'd also put here the Spirit Without Name from the Players Guide. Now the totem descriptions says some of these only accept Lupus members as pack totem. But that doesn't mean the spirits themselves can't be summoned or interact by other breeds. Totem bans are supposed to be intrinsic, not by choice. So they can still be friendly to all Garou even if they can only be pack totems to a certain group.<br /> <br /> What other candidates do we have? On the principle that Wolf's spirit brood is friendly and available to all Garou potentially, we can look at those pack totems that seem universally popular as opposed to tribe or regionally specific. The corebook totems are all accounted for, but we have expanded lists in the Players Guide that should still fit this principle. Not every totem listed there seems appropriate. But we have some totems that showed up in every Player's Guide, and some that seem especially appropriate for Wolf. I think Weasel and Wolverine are good choices. Possibly Flea - not an immediate choice, but there's logic to it. Fog (Aeolus) is present in every guide, and I'm inclined to add it just to explain why it's so popular among Garou even though it's not an obvious choice by itself. It would also help to diversify the brood.<br /> <br /> There's also the option of Phoenix although a good case can be made it should be its own special Incarna not part of any other spirit's brood. In its favor is Phoenix's special role in Garou culture - the source of the Prophecy of Phoenix, and its role as the totem for the Silver Pack. It could also be connected with the idea that the First Wolf saved Gaia from death, died in doing so, but Gaia raised him back to life. That fits thematically in Garou myth. But I could also see the point who would want Phoenix to be separate. So I'm undecided currently.<br /> <br /> Another &quot;obvious&quot; addition would be Raven, but Raven is already spread thin with having its own children (Corax), and multiple examples existing in other broods. As much as it makes sense to add him, the game is forcing us to keep him separate. Though it's possible to add another special group of Raven spirits just for Wolf that embody the symbiotic role between ravens and wolves in their hunting/feeding practices. Maybe call these spirits &quot;Wolf Birds&quot;. If you have a better name suggestion, please share.<br /> <br /> Wolf may not have a very large brood, but that is OK. The size of the brood alone does not determine the Incarna's power. Stag is known for having a large brood, but Owl has a small brood. Both are still major Incarna.<br /> <br /> Are there any obvious choices I missed? Other spirits that would make sense? Other comments on the brood and its role in the Umbra and connection to the Garou?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-07T00:10:25-08:00","id":1434735,"title":"","content":"At this point, I want to go into more detail about how I intend to use this in my chronicles. What benefits does this get me? I did not do this before in my games, so why am I doing it now? Well, I want to improve my depiction of the Umbra and spirits in general. By organizing things, I better understand the spirit world, and thus can better describe things to the PCs. This is just one of the ways I am doing so.<br /> <br /> The main reason is to give me a tool that helps me differentiate the spirits when they interact with the PCs. Assigning them to a brood gives me several things that help me out. First, it defines the spirit's loyalties. That may come into play. Second, it could also give me rivals, and that could affect the PCs. Pegasus doesn't like Fenris, so it would make sense if a Get of Fenris interacting with one of Pegasus' brood has more troubles than other Garou. Not all spirits have obvious issues with another, but some have things that STs can explore (Bear and Turtle are obvious, but potentially so do any of the totems of the other Changing Breeds or fallen tribes.) Third, association with a brood should give it some affinity with the desires of the major Incarna, so it helps me as an ST when coming up with motivations, chiminage requests and the like. It also helps me as an ST determine what kind of spirits care about certain events the most. If I want a spirit to introduce a plot hook, I can pick a brood that is most impacted by it, and pick a spirit from it.<br /> <br /> It also helps to create consequences for PC actions in the Umbra. It gives me a quick way to handle spirit affinity and notoriety. I can't recall if tabletop materials go into this, but I know several LARP organizations use the concept as a means to systematize Garou's involvement with spirits. Things you do either build your reputation with the spirits, or hurt your reputation. Some things Garou do should build affinity and notoriety in general. But there are times when actions may build reputation with one brood, but not affect others, or even harm reputation with another. Knowing major broods in advance allows me to do this.<br /> <br /> This creates additional roleplay opportunities for the PCs, especially Theurges. I have traditionally handled things very generically without giving much thought to distinguishing spirits. Now I have a framework the Theurge needs to navigate, just like in the real world. They can make friends and enemies. They can choose to specialize in one kind of spirit brood, expand their connection, or attempt to make all happy. Each approach has benefits and drawbacks.<br /> <br /> It can also help PCs when they build their character. They can utilize this approach in Backgrounds like Spirit Heritage or Spirit Network, and the ST can help them with that. Or with Merits and Flaws like Enemy, Notoriety, or tied to a Curse.<br /> <br /> It also helps me make quick determinations on how likely spirits might know the Garou Tongue and choose to speak with them. For example, the broods of the Tribal Totems should know them and should usually be willing to speak it with Garou. But the brood of Turtle, while knowing the language as a result of their association with the Croatan, might choose to not to speak it because of Turtle turning its back on them. While the brood of Bear might now know it at all. This helps an ST navigate the ways that non-Theurges can interact with spirits, but still keep an important role for Theurges to use Spirit Speech.<br /> <br /> Another thing it does for me is give me a handy list of major spirits that should be the \u201cpantheon\u201d of the Garou and Changing Breeds. That comes in handy when I want to create a myth or legend for the game. (This is another thing I am working on \u2013 a list of short, handy tales I can introduce in game, especially as a way to teach about the Umbra. I am fond of using very short stories \u2013 like Aesop's Fables, or shortened versions of Kipling's Just So Stories \u2013 to explain the spirit world.) If I'm telling a myth about a council of the animal spirits, I know who to mention and who likely shouldn't be mentioned. I've found that brief, short stories of this kind don't take up much time in game, and the players tend to like them a lot.<br /> <br /> How complicated an ST wants to make this is up to them. Theoretically, an ST could reduce the broods \u2013 at least in terms of RP tool \u2013 to a very few. Or they could make it even more varied than I am. Whatever helps them. But I do think this approach offers benefits to an ST, especially one who has mastered other elements of the game, and now want to work on their handling of the Umbra.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-07T00:12:44-08:00","id":1434736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>pwtucker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1410223-additional-spirit-broods?p=1434280#post1434280\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lot of fascinating stuff here. Will read again, think about it some, then respond.</div> </div> </div> </div>Looking forward to any comments. Doing this allows me to brainstorm and organize my thoughts, but hearing other views would give me perspective or even inspiration.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-09T15:50:42-08:00","id":1435018,"title":"","content":"At this point, a slight digression. Rather than go into detail about one particular spirit brood, I want to talk about category of them \u2013 the Incarna of the seas.<br /> <br /> As a group, they likely have little relevance to most Werewolf chronicles. There may be some very specific chronicle concepts where they come into play, or if one is running a Rokea chronicle, but usually they don't need to be addressed at all, or at best very loosely. Since we likely won't need an extensive list of such spirits, we don't need to create large broods for every Incarna. If we just have a group of appropriate Incarna, we can just slot any spirits as needed. Hence just one post for the lot of them.<br /> <br /> Naturally there is not much information in the sourcebooks, but the most relevant are the Rokea book and Blood-Dimmed Tides. But we do have mention of various marine animals scattered elsewhere. So we can construct a basic system of Major Incarna here.<br /> <br /> First edition Players Guide has <b>Old Man Sea</b> as a pack totem. He never gets mentioned again. He's likely not a popular totem among Garou, but I think the game misses something without him. Old Man Sea should be a figure equivalent to Grandfather Thunder \u2013 a Sea Lord to the Sky Father like Poseidon to Zeus. He's a good place to store whales and dolphins, and any iconic sea creatures and plants that wouldn't fit elsewhere like starfish, seahorses, seaweed, kelp, and the like. Marine mammals like walruses and seals can also go here unless you want them as their own thing.<br /> <br /> <b>Shark</b> is another obvious candidate. He is the father of the Rokea, and he's mentioned as a pack totem in several Players Guides. His spirit brood would involve any large marine predators \u2013 most likely other fish like marlins, swordfish, albacore.<br /> <br /> Blood-Dimmed Tides gives us an explicit Incarna \u2013 Pakurwu Tooth Edged, the Incarna of Coral and Reef. I can't find any reference to the name Pakurwu, so it is either made up or a derivative of something. I would just call her <b>Coral</b>. Any of the critters that depend on the reefs as an ecosystem would fall under her brood.<br /> <br /> We know <b>Turtle</b> is an Incarna as well, although one that has either grown distant completely or just distant from the Garou. But he still needs a brood. As a former Garou totem, his brood would include lots of non-marine animals and concepts. But he would include a lot of marine animals like turtles and other amphibians. One possibility is to include seabirds here as well \u2013 though these might be their own brood or part of Old Man Sea, or maybe under Falcon.<br /> <br /> I also want to mention <b>Uktena</b>, as he is the most likely candidate to be an Incarna of freshwater animals \u2013 we know spirits of fish, river monsters, and sea serpents in general are part of his brood. So if we ever need other similar creatures of the fresh waters \u2013 but not marine animals \u2013 this is a good place to stick them if Turtle isn't the right place. Sulis, or Thermal Spring, is an interesting totem in Revised Players Guide that should go somewhere. I'm undecided whether it belongs here or with Turtle, or perhaps some other place.<br /> <br /> At this point, we've run out of obvious candidates in published sources, but we can easily see gaps. An Incarna of Ocotpi and Squid is natural. The Rokera version of Banes are called Qyrlings, and they often take the form of cephalapods and jellyfish. But it's obvious these animals are not Banes, and Qyrling merely assume the form of them, they aren't them themselves. Blood-Dimmed Tides call all spirits of sea creatures Apsarae, and divide them into Bone and Unboned (Rokea gives us the term Pelageans for spirits of all fish and marine mammals). So there needs to be at least one Incarna for the Unboned Apsarae. An obvious name would be Kraken, but that name is already used as an alternative form of Qyrl/Wyrm. I'd just call it <b>Squid</b> and get on with it. A lot of the molluscs and other Unboned (like jellyfish and the like) can go here. You might want to use some other spirit.<br /> <br /> You might also want an Incarna of the various crustacheons \u2013 they're Unboned as well, but I think most of us would want them to be separate from the spirits of squids and octopi. Crab is mentioned as pack (slew in the term of Rokea) totem in Rokea, and horseshoe crab in Mokole. So let's make <b>Crab</b> the Incarna of this brood. As a side note, Weaver spirits take the form of shelled animals, much like how they take the form of spiders on land. They equivalent to Pattern Spiders as a group are called Scuttlers, but these are Weaver spirits \u2013 drawn to ships, oil rigs, and the like \u2013 not Gaian spirits.<br /> <br /> Depending on how far you want to diversify things, I'm sure there are all sorts of gaps you could fill to get an even larger array of Major Incarna. But unless you are a marine biologist or planning an extensive sea chronicle, it's unlikely you need anything more than this. Still, I think there is a role for at least another Incarna of fish. Forage fish are those smaller prey fish that get eaten by larger ones, and they are an important source for the fishing industry. As a prey species, it fits a similar role that Stag does on land. So I think adding one more here makes sense. The largest group of such fish are the Clupeidae which include herring, sardines, anchovies, and the like. They're often called the herring family, so <b>Herring</b> it is. Still, if you just want to dump this under Old Man Sea, it would also fit and I might do the same. Same thing for all those other strange fish \u2013 rays, eels, lampreys, and the like. If it is important enough to you, make a new Incarna. If not, stick them somewhere else.<br /> <br /> We also have oddball spirits found among the other Incarna. Axis Mundi put Narwhal under Unicorn because narwhals have a single protuding tusk that makes it a horned animal like Unicorn. And maybe you want Salmon under Stag or Bear. Otters are mentioned under Wendigo and Unicorn. Polar Bears are obviously under Bear. Oysters and clams might be put under Mole as diggers of the soil if you want more diversity. So there's lots of options.<br /> <br /> And of course you have all sorts of variants of water-spirits you could put under the Water Elemental (I don't think we've ever been given names of elemental Incarnas, but there are plenty of inspiration we can take from myth and legend). And there seems to be a lot of Wyld spirits that are similar \u2013 Breakers mentioned in Rokea, and Spiral Schools in Blood-Dimmed Tides. Both have the Charm Break Reality which is a Wyld charm. These groups should be rarely encountered unless you are running a chronicle completely centered on the ocean, or someplace exotic like the Arctic Circle.<br /> <br /> This should be more than enough for any Garou game, and probably superfluous for 98% of them. But if you set a game in a coastal city or near a shore, it might be useful to have some groupings of these spirits. Even in freshwater locations \u2013 like around the Great Lakes \u2013 an ST may still want to distinguish between those spirits loyal to Uktena, Turtle, and invasive species of Old Man Sea plus any oddball stuff. Or if you want the PCs to go after the PENTEX subsidiary Hallahan Fishing and at some point they need to learn how to navigate the very different umbral broods most affected by them.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-11T21:18:58-08:00","id":1435269,"title":"","content":"This post will be less detailed than others because it's something I am still kicking around in my head. And that is the issue of Plant based Major Incarna with their own broods.<br /> <br /> Plant spirits are <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1431536-plant-spirits\">a major part of the game with their own rules</a>, and they can play a major role for the PCs as natural talens when plants are awakened by the Rite of Spirit Awakening. Axis Mundi not only gives us a bunch of plant spirits, but also details some plant spirits among the tribal incarna (olive trees for Unicorn; grain for Stag). Croatan Song lists Corn Maiden as a Garou totem, though it defines it as a spirit affiliated with the Weaver (as presumably are all domesticated crops). That might place it under Turtle. Rage Across Russia also lists a lot of spirits for &quot;streams and fields&quot; (although some of these could be Wraiths or Fae, but shown as spirits for ease of play) that also fit well under plant spirits. The original Wendigo Tribebook listed certain plant spirits that were &quot;natural&quot; Talens (not needing to be awakened for their spirit powers to take effect) such as tobacco and purifying plants like sage, cedar, and sweet grass.<br /> <br /> Axis Mundi also tells us that Oak, like the other tree spirits, serves the Incarna called the Cosmic Tree (also known as many other names like Tree of Life, World Tree, etc.) It also tells us that Oak formed an alliance with Stag. So we know there is at least one plant spirit with its own brood - the World Tree. Now the World Tree has many more spiritual correspondences than just being a plant. So I think this means we should be thinking very big in terms of what kind of plants should qualify as a major Incarna with its own brood.<br /> <br /> There are some very powerful psychedelic plant totems in Axis Mundi, but they are referred to as enigmatic spirits. And I personally think such enigmatic spirits, despite their power, have their own brood. That would make them much less enigmatic. We might also want to place Tobacco here as a very powerful plant spirit that just does not have its own brood.<br /> <br /> So we know there is World Tree, under whom we can place most other tree spirits and presumably a lot of others plants in the forest (and possibly some spirits that aren't plants). Are there any other possible candidates? None that I know of from the sourcebooks. But we might create a few more on plants that seem they should have strong spiritual power.<br /> <br /> One possible candidate in my mind would be Rose. Flowers in general are distinguished from other plants, and many in general - particularly roses - have strong symbolic value of many different meanings.<br /> <br /> Another possibility might be Mistletoe. It's a parasitic plant which makes it different from others. It had strong connections with fertility, vitality, and protection from evil. The Christmas ritual of kissing under the mistletoe, and the legend of Loki causing Baldur's death from a mistletoe arrow also speak to its power. It also has medicinal value and some important ecological roles. I'm not sure what kind of Incarna or spirit brood it could be, but ruling over many medicinal herbs and such is one possibility as well as plants available in winter to feed animals. Although I admit there are many other plants that could fulfill the same role.<br /> <br /> Should domesticated plants have their own Incarna? And what different role would they have? In Axis Mundi, they discuss Corn spirit. And while the description is that of maize, it also talks about how Gaia sometimes adopt the guise of a corn spirit (meaning grains in general like wheat or rye). This is something that usually confuses American readers. Europeans often use the term &quot;corn&quot; to mean any kind of grain, but Americans use the term to mean exclusively maize. So I think the reference to European corn spirits mean this is grain in general (while the Grain spirit of Stag seems to refer to grains used to make alcoholic drinks, not those that are eaten - making this even more confusing). Anyway, in that description of Corn, Axis Mundi tells us that while Corn was a major source of Gaian worship with various harvest festivals and the like, that the spirit is far less available now since people celebrate that less and the rise of industrial agriculture has transformed the harvest into a far less spiritual endeavor. Still, I think &quot;Corn&quot; (we may want to call this Cereal to make it less confusing) could be a good selection. As an Incarna of domesticated crops, it is clearly segregated from other plants and has a very unique depiction that would make plants of this kind treated very differently from all wild ones.<br /> <br /> We might also want to look at this from other perspectives. If World Tree covers trees, then it obviously is a spirit of forests. But we have other biomes like tundra (both polar and alpine), grasslands, and deserts. These are dominated by other kinds of plants. And wetlands could be considered their own thing. Should one or more of these have their own Incarna to rule over the plants of these habitats? Are there any real candidates of spiritual power among the wild grasses, shrubs, reeds, and such that could act as a Major Incarna with their own brood?<br /> <br /> Garou games are unlikely to utilize Plant incarna or their spirit broods much. I rarely see Garou PCs interact with plant spirits, and those that do seem to stick mainly to using the Rite of Spirit Awakening. There is little interaction even to make fetishes, making chiminage relationships unlikely. And while it is possible to imagine some specialized games utilizing the Incarna of the seas or waters, it would be a very unique game that made extensive use of plant spirits.<br /> <br /> But plants are a vital part of Earth's ecosystem, and the idea of Garou as protectors of the landscape are built into the game. So this could be an area where creative thoughts could generate a lot of good ideas that could vitalize many chronicles and open up a richer experience. I think this could be an area of very beneficial creative brainstorming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-12T10:28:08-08:00","id":1435304,"title":"","content":"There was an issue of Swamp Thing where Alec had to tell a funny story, so he told a tale of how he snuck into a movie theatre to surreptitiously watch a movie he really loved. He used a piece of popcorn to corn a very tiny corncob-body. A later issue showed Swamp Thing and the rest of his kind marching to war, and, amidst all the awesome imagery is corncob Swamp Thing.<br /> <br /> Now I want to play a corncob kami charged with helping out a farmer...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12219-demigod-beast","name":"Demigod Beast","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T21:14:45-08:00","id":1436139,"title":"","content":"Hmm, think this last post was meant for the kami thread... Oh, well.<br /> <br /> I next want to look at reptile spirits. The game has had many of them over the years, and besides the Croatan tribal totem of Turtle, there are also the Mokole and Nagah who are shapechangers. So there should be some Major Incarna with their own spirit broods out there. <br /> <br /> I originally thought to start at a trio of totems looking at Turtle, Crocodile, and Snake. But after borrowing the Mokole and Nagah tribebooks to look at what they mention about totems and spirits in general, I had to make changes. It seems the Nagah have the exact same patrons as the Mokole \u2013 the Dragon Kings. So that folded everything with them into one spirit brood. I wasn't too disappointed because I had trouble finding appropriate spirits to fill under Snake other than... snakes.<br /> <br /> One reason I am very interested in these spirit broods is that they really represent a different roleplay opportunity than the others. While not outright hostile to Garou (Garou packs still have many totems that are found here), these are likely some of the most unfriendly overall of all the Gaian spirits. The Turtle incarna is noticeably absent. And the Mokole and Nagah are some of the more hostile Fera towards the Garou. So we have the challenge of determining some guidelines for the spirits of these broods to show this while still allowing Garou to obtain pack totems and engage in chiminage.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T21:17:11-08:00","id":1436141,"title":"","content":"Let's start with <b>the Dragon Kings</b>. I must admit I am not fond of that name. The term dragon has a lot of connotations I don't think truly represents these spirits. And I disagree with a lot of the backstory and description created for the Mokole (there is a certain kind of people who thinks weredinosaurs are very cool \u2013 I am not one of them). At the very least, I think the Garou would use a different name even if the Mokole and Nagah don't. I personally would like something else that could be a placeholder for a lord of reptiles. Maybe the Great Reptile/Lizard/Serpent. Or the King of Snakes mentioned in the Bastet book. Or perhaps a mythic name like Draco, whose constellation is that of a serpent, but it isn't clear what mythological creature it is supposed to be so we can use that ambiguity. If I had to pick an actual animal, my preference would definitely be Crocodile. I don't know what I'll pick for my own chronicles right now (though leaning to just Crocodile, at least for the purposes of the name the Garou use for it). But for the purposes of this thread, I'll stick to the Dragon Kings for convenience.<br /> <br /> The Dragon Kings are clearly the patrons of the Mokole and Nagah. They are associated with most reptiles, the sun, longevity and memory, and venomous creatures. The Nagah ONLY have associations with spirits of the Dragon Kings because they will keep their secrets, and eschew connections with other spirits. And Mokole generally don't have mammalian totems, but do share many totems with Garou that aren't mammals. Though some spirit totems aligned with Mokole won't have anything to do with Garou. So off the bat, we know we should exclude mammal spirits and the like here. But we could still pick spirits of birds, maybe some insects, and perhaps even sea creatures as well.<br /> <br /> There's actually a very long list of existing Garou totems we can include. They are Chameleon, Adder, Anaconda, Rattlesnake, Cobra, and Crocodile. Mokole book mentions Quetzal as a common totem as well. And the same book mentions a lot of Mokole specific totems like Dragon, Finch, Scarab, Snake Doctor (a dragonfly \u2013 the term comes from some American Indians), Whiptail Lizard, the Last Ones, and Horseshoe Crab (though this last one could fit into several broods). This is a fairly substantial list, and most of these spirits don't have obvious homes in other broods. Bastet also mentions Old Snapjaw which is another Crocodile spirit, and Uktena lists both Gila Monster and Dragonfly. Though these two may not be part of Uktena's brood, but totems open to Uktena because of that totem's affinity with the Dragon Kings.<br /> <br /> Collectively, this is a very risky brood for Garou to deal with. Some are available as pack totems, but others will refuse to have anything to do with Garou. I think most of these will not know the Garou Tongue at all, and those that do would only use it with their own pack children. That puts a barrier between them and Garou, and would force Garou to utilize Theurges with Spirit Speech. We could say that even those pack totems might treat their Garou children well, but might be more hostile to other Garou. They are likely to not reveal certain secrets to the Garou at all (not even their pack children)... except maybe to the Uktena because of the nature of that tribal totem. That actually works well with that tribe's mystique. And the Silent Striders may also have some better look because of their association with Crocodile.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-19T21:19:06-08:00","id":1436142,"title":"","content":"Turtle's brood also offers us some distinction. While most reptiles will go with the Dragon Kings, <b>Turtle</b> probably has the amphibians as well as many amphibious animals that aren't reptiles. Turtle is associated with stability, patience, longevity, and armor. Turtles are also liminal animals that cross the boundary between land and water. Shelled creatures also have a natural home here \u2013 so while I can't find printed spirits like armadillo or snail (or any shelled gastropod like abalone and conches), we can include them here. Croatan Song gives us Earth Burrowers, Trout, and Corn Maiden. Trout's fine, and we can probably include some other freshwater fish (though these are also found elsewhere). Earth Burrowers seems to fill some of the category we assigned to Mole and his Tillers of the Soil, but there's no problem in at least some of those spirits being here (or you may want to ditch that group entirely and dump all of them here). Clams \u2013 which are shelled, aquatic and which burrow in the ground \u2013 would be particularly appropriate. As would the nautilus \u2013 another aquatic shelled mollusc. Corn Maiden could be an issue if you want to keep that spirit separate into a Plant based brood, or it could just be a special version of the spirit \u2013 there's lot of examples like that in the game.<br /> <br /> Are there other good candidates? I like the idea of putting Beaver here. I really like its totem description, and it's an iconic animal. I think it would have a good home with turtle. It's a semiaquatic animal, and I think it's instinct at building dams fits in well too. Another semiaquatic animal is Otter. Different Otter totems are mentioned in several places in the game (Children of Gaia and Wendigo), but there's no reason another can't be here. Some animal spirits seem to like having multiple brood homes. And as a Garou totem, turtle should have a some more diversity than the Dragon Kings. Salamander (from Rage Across Appalachia) and the various Frog and Toad totems also have a natural home here, but I think at least some of these creatures could be found in other broods as well (I previously mentioned some of these \u2013 especially those associated with Healing \u2013 might be found among Bear's brood).<br /> <br /> I also think Gull from the Silent Striders tribebook best fits here. While I think many of the totems in the tribebooks best fit as brood members of the tribal incarna, that is not a universal rule. Some spirits are mentioned in multiple places, or obviously belong elsewhere. I would say that while Gull (and many other seabirds) belongs to Turtle, that Gull is also specifically friendly to the Striders as well. And while we have Crab as its own Incarna and brood in out post on sea totems, I don't mind putting hermit crabs here \u2013 they aren't true crabs and their taking over of mollusc shells as a portable home puts them closer to Turtle.<br /> <br /> We might also want to include any of the aquatic insects among Turtle \u2013 those that live in water for part of their lifecycle, especially as eggs. These include dragonflies (we might say that Uktena and Mokole have access to them as totem as a result of their ties to the Croatan or Turtle), but there are lots of others like water beetles, whirligigs, springtails, etc.. Maybe the more friendly or newer species are part of Turtle, while the less friendly or older species are part of the Dragon Kings. Some might even be part of Uktena's brood, especially if they have myths or legends about them as mysterious or magical creatures. So STs could stick them in several broods depending on what makes sense to them. And there's no reason why these spirits can't be in multiple broods.<br /> <br /> Another group would be a select group of semiaquatic plants like mangroves, pond cypress, salt marsh grasses, reeds, and of course the iconic lilypad. Not all turtles are amphibious. Many tortoises live solely on land and even in the desert (though here they burrow to escape the heat during the day), so perhaps some desert shrubs and such thorny plants could also fit.<br /> <br /> Roleplaying Turtle's brood is a different challenge that the Dragon Kings. Turtle was once a strong ally of the Garou, but the lost of the Croatan seem to have caused it to withdraw favor (from them, but perhaps not from other Breeds like the Mokole). But that can't have applied to most of its brood who seem to still act as pack totems. But Turtle's withdraw nature, disappointment, and possible harano should be indicated somehow through its spirit brood. Perhaps they notice their Incarna protection's absence, or are more likely to criticize their Garou children in their failing to act. This breed likely knows the Garou Tongue, but might refuse to speak it in general as a result of Turtle's disappointment (again, excepting a pack totem's children, and individual Garou who have established a good rapport/chiminage with individual spirits). As an individual Garou builds his specific reputation/affinity with the brood and his reputation spreads to other spirits in the brood, the spirit brood would become more friendly with that specific Garou and begin using the Garou Tongue.<br /> <br /> Like always, I wish I had the creativity to come up with truly novel spirits like some of those found in Axis Mundi to populate the broods, but that's just something I'll need to work on.<br /> <br /> I'm sure if I tried harder, I would find more candidates for reptile based major incarna and broods, but for now I'll stick with two.<br /> <br /> At some point, my intention is to come up a list of major incarna and spirit broods, each with a brief description and roleplay guide. Something that can fit on one typed paged as an easy and quick reference to help me roleplay these spirits. Right now I have lot more than when I first started this thread. So I am hoping some people out there are finding this as useful as I am.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-20T22:17:03-08:00","id":1436371,"title":"","content":"Now I come to the Bastet looking for information as there clearly needs to be one or more major incarna based on the big cats. However, just like I originally conceived that both Nagah and Mokole would lead me to two different reptile spirit broods, I thought I'd probably have one spirit brood based on each kind of Bastet. However, after reading through the Bastet tribebook and their original description in the Players Guide, I now think one spirit brood covers it.<br /> <br /> The game's lore does a very good job of tying these various cat breeds together with its own set of mythology and legends (I think the Bastet book is by far the best of all breedbooks. It's excellent.). Each breed is supposed to be descended from the same progenitors, and they all have a common mythology. Just need to figure out who the Major Incarna should be. The obvious choices would be either the King of Cats, Mistress of Catkind, or the King of Beasts. <br /> <br /> I finally settled on King of Beasts because it had the highest totem cost in the Jamak section, and the Jamark description was very clear that each level of rank indicated a more powerful spirit type. Level 5 was for \u201ctotem spirit\u201d. And King of Beasts was level 6 (indicating, I presume, that it was powerful enough to be a pride/pack totem, not just an individual Bastet). It is clearly a Lion spirit. The description clearly says Garou packs are some allies of the totem, indicating this is the Lion totem in some fashion.<br /> <br /> \u201cLion\u201d has a lot of baggage in Werewolf. It was its own Garou totem for the White Howlers, and that totem is now said to be part of Griffin's brood. Obviously that spirit can't be a major incarna with its own brood. So regardless whether one says the Garou Lion is actually \u201ccave lion\u201d or merely an \u201caspect\u201d of Lion but not the true Lion, we need to be careful.<br /> <br /> From a Bastet perspective, I am perfectly fine with calling this Incarna King of Beasts. But I doubt the Garou would do so. Such a name would imply Lion is superior to Wolf, and Garou myth (which may not be shared by other Changing Breeds) puts Wolf as Gaia's favorite and all other spirits owe him reverence (this is why the Garou alone have tribes with other spirits as their patrons). So instead I will use the term <b>Wildcat</b> in my chronicles \u2013 the lord of all cats. It is generic enough name and his brood can include all the spirits of the individual cat breeds of the Bastet. The Garou totem \u201clion\u201d just has a very peculiar history/mythology as originally a spirit under Wildcat/King of Beasts and then a major incarna in its own right as patron of the White Howlers, and now under Griffin. (And somehow there is a separate Lion spirit under Wildcat as well. Either Lion is a big enough concept that it has two aspects, or the Garou Lion is actually a separate spirit of the extinct Cave Lions. Use whichever one you like best.)<br /> <br /> Appropriately, there are not many pack totems obviously associated with Wildcat. I could only find two big cat totems \u2013 Panther in Black Furies tribebook and Jaguar in Rage Across Australia. And both of them make references to the War of Rage and troubles between the totem and most Garou. (Jaguar's ban even states its children must fight other Garou.) And an interesting thing about \u201cPanther\u201d is that panther is actually a \u201c<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panther_(legendary_creature)\" target=\"_blank\">legendary creature</a>\u201d like Griffin or Unicorn, not an actual species of big cat! It might be that Furies have access to this totem precisely because it isn't the spirit of a real breed, but a legendary spirit more open to allying with certain Garou despite its allegiance to Wildcat. To these we could add any number of other cat spirits representing the tribes of Bastet and other felines. These are all obvious choices.<br /> <br /> Other spirits easy to slot here would be other feliforms (catlike animals) in Carnivora \u2013 hyenas and mongoose are likely well known, but also animals in the viverridae, eupleridae and more rare orders. Incidentally, placing Hyena as one of the spirit brood of Wildcat solves the issue of the Ajaba being involved with the Bastet despite not being a cat, just like sticking Jackal or Thylacine under Wolf can explain how werejackals and weremarsupials are part of the Garou Nation. This is an unexpected benefit of approaching things from a spirit brood perspective.<br /> <br /> Another group of spirits found here are the Jamak \u2013 the special spirit allies of the Bastet that are defined as \u201cminor Gafflings of larger totem Incarna\u201d in the Bastet tribebook. This is categorically different than pack totems who are called \u201cjaggling avatars\u201d of the tribal incarna. Jamak include a wide variety of spirits \u2013 vultures, elephants, butterflies, and various cat spirits are all included. Although not detailed, the book also includes a list of notable Jamak of snakes, spiders, rhinos, hippos, cranes, hawks, and more. Most have unique \u201cpersonal names\u201d rather than universal titles (Bonyscrap rather than Vulture), so it may be a clue they are distinct (category of) spirits rather than true universal spirits of those animals. So rather than these spirits being normal types that are part of Wildcat's brood, I think it's best to just say Jamak are their own special category which include many diverse spirits.<br /> <br /> There are lots of African animals not obviously part of an existing brood \u2013 elephants, hippos, giraffes antelopes, zebras, rhinos (if they are not part of Unicorn's or Bear's brood), lemurs, aardvarks, water buffalo, monkeys, apes, ostriches, and so on. They have to fit somewhere. But none scream out to me that they belong under Wildcat. Some of them could be. But others might fit under a theoretical brood under Elephant (any number of large animals) or under another totem (like antelopes under Stag). Many of the Bastet breeds are represented in Africa, so this is a disappointment I can't find some to put under Wildcat. But if you have suggestions, let me know.<br /> <br /> Besides cats and related animals, Wildcat has correspondences with curiosity, nine-lives, secrets, hiding, gracefulness, and hunting prowess (usually solitary). In theory, this provides a good basis to assign or create additional spirits of the brood. Unfortunately, I can't think of any right now. The sheer number of other broods have taken up a lot of spirits. I'll have to be much more creative later to come up with them. A legendary spirit like manticore could be one of them. In the meantime, feel free to list your choices for inclusion.<br /> <br /> However, this does lead me to an insight. One reason the Garou are so dominant, perhaps why they won the War of Rage, is the sheer number of spirit allies they have as a result of their tribes. LOTS of spirits are prone to be favorable to them, and this gives them a great advantage that other Changing Breeds don't have.<br /> <br /> Wildcat's brood is fairly powerful, but especially targeted towards helping the Bastet. Less useful to other Fera. They are not favorable towards Garou, but some are willing to help certain packs provided they pay a price. These should not know the Garou Tongue and be initially unfriendly, perhaps even hostile. This is a good roleplaying hook for STs when running spirits here. Many spirit patrons of other Changing Breeds like Boar and Bear don't seem to be hostile to Garou despite what happened to their children. So it's good to have one Gaian brood that is on the hostile end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-25T00:18:59-08:00","id":1436950,"title":"","content":"Let's take a moment to consider Axis Mundi which did more than any other book to develop the idea of spirit broods and showcase examples of them. The book makes clear a brood is composed of gafflings, more powerful jagglings, and even lesser incarna. Generic spirits are gafflings while individually named spirits are either jagglings or incarna. As we'll see, there are also groups of jagglings described in the broods.<br /> <br /> Each of the broods were written by different authors, and it shows. Some broods are clearly better written than others.<br /> <br /> I think a comparison to the theoretical broods I constructed would be beneficial. It could point out places where I may have been wrong, and when it seems to fit. I can't do all broods at once, so I'll take them one at a time.<br /> <br /> <b>Chimera </b>is defined simply as a totem of dreams and whatever spirits she wants in her brood, mostly dealing with wisdom and enlightenment. Some Epiphlings are in her brood, but appear only in dreams while other spirits can appear in both dreams and the normal umbra.<br /> <br /> We're only given four spirits \u2013 one is a unique jaggling, another lists a jaggling leader and his gaffling servants, one a group of jagglings, and the last gafflings. They're sufficiently different and evocative, but unlikely to come into play unless a chronicle is heavy into dreams. I think they best serve as creative examples of spirits an ST can create herself for broods. Beltsu and Aralin are just OK. The Patchwork Wolf is a good example of a unique jaggling that could play a role in a spirit quest. But I think the Dream Ravens have the most potential to be used in chronicles.<br /> <br /> These are good spirits, but four is just too little. We need some generic dream spirits as well, though I assume we can take some Chimmerlings from the corebook. However, it does a good job letting us know Chimera is primarily a spirit of dreams, with little concrete correspondences in the material world. This is a tight focus, but there's nothing wrong with Chimera being primarily a totem of dreams, one of the Oneiri.<br /> <br /> (Outside of spirit brood issues, the description of the Dream Ravens establishes there in fact very rare \u201cindependent\u201d Stargazers (Garou devoted to Chimera) in the Americas along with the Three Brothers. Like a lot of things mentioned once in the game in a minor way, I believe this never got developed. It opens the possibility of members of various tribes being presented in the &quot;wrong&quot; hemisphere, but isolated from their brethren and not realizing the have many more members.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 02-25-2021, 03:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-25T09:52:01-08:00","id":1437006,"title":"","content":"With a totem like Chimera, there may be an overlap of dream-spirits of the Middle Umbra with chimera from the Dreaming. Garou might accidentally lump all Dreaming chimera as Chimera\u2019s brood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12219-demigod-beast","name":"Demigod Beast","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1154"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-25T13:38:04-08:00","id":1437037,"title":"","content":"It's possible, but the Garou seem to have a clear conception of the fae distinct from spirits (even if we're now told that Arcadia Gateway has &quot;spirits&quot; rather than true &quot;fae&quot;). It's the Fianna and not the Stargazers who know the most about the fae (and the fae includes all the various changeling kiths, other kiths, and all the fae chimerae) which would not be the case if Chimera was their lord. Using the same term over and over again to different things does get confusing though.<br /> <br /> The totem Chimera is about real dreams and enigmas. It's about enlightenment and understanding. When the Changeling game talks about &quot;dreams&quot; they generally mean it as euphemism for imagination and creativity which can involve actual dreams, but mostly includes active creativity by people who are awake, not sleeping.<br /> <br /> But there probably are some spirits that are part of Chimera's brood that do manifest somehow in the Dreaming. Some of these might be those fae chimera that are actually created by mortals' sleeping and dreaming. I think it's OK for certain spirits to show up in different &quot;Umbras&quot;/planes.<br /> <br /> I think most Garou are likely very ignorant about the spirit realms of dreams and Chimera in general. But Stargazers, knowledgeable theurges, and experienced spirit travelers likely have an understanding that Chimera is just one of the Dreamlords, and the Dream Zone is large and weird with lots of things that don't owe Chimera any allegiance.<br /> <br /> I think the Werewolf game has undeveloped rules and setting information for dreams. The other Dreamlords aren't detailed. We're not given ideas to the kind of things that can happen in Chimares, or other spirits that can be encountered there. It's all left to the individual imaginations of the STs. There should be more official support.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-25T21:49:13-08:00","id":1437082,"title":"","content":"<b>Cockroach</b> recruits spirits for their cunning and survival instincts, and his brood are primarily about protecting Cockroach's influence. That's a very utilitarian perspective and one centered around Cockroach's power \u2013 not the defense of Gaia. That's my interpretation of what is written, but it gives good RP guidance to STs. It distinguishes Cockroach from the other Garou totems. All totems want power, but that is generally not their primary motivation. Then again they also aren't a small insect that is despised and hated by everyone.<br /> <br /> The brood is a mixed brood of naturae and triatic spirits that help Cockroach. Five spirits are described - 2 jagglings and 3 gafflings. However, several other spirits of Cockroch are name dropped which an ST could easily expand on. One annoying thing here is that a lot of nonsense words are used to name these spirits. It gets confusing as you forget whether this spirit is the kilakac'n or mula'krante, and whether it was the kir'kakay who created one of them or vice versa or if that was the mu'jabber instead. The writer would have done better to have a more pronounceable and understandable name. They just get in the way. Some of the name dropped brood spirits \u2013 like Spider-Wasps \u2013 sound more interesting than what was published. The easiest to remember are the Scab Birds - spirits of birds that live in cities (the scabs per most Garou) like pigeons.<br /> <br /> It's a diverse brood with utilitarian use. Any city based chronicle should be making use of these spirits. But unfortunately few of these spirits really scream Cockroach to me. Instead of being the definitive spirits of the brood, they seem more like interesting flavor. The Glass Walkers seem to me a strong concept in general, but the specific ideas behind them never seem to quite come together. This brood is the same way. Except for the nonsense names, the brood is not bad. It just seems incomplete and does not cover things it should.<br /> <br /> (Incidentally, I think the key concept Cockroach needs to give its Garou tribes are the traits of commensalism (animals living in long term symbiosis where one group sgain benefits while the other is neither benefited nor harmed) and hiding among humans. This is critical for any group of Garou who interact with large groups of humans like the Walkers. These traits would have been immediately useful even in the earliest times. Connections to the Weaver would have come later as an emergent property dealing with the strange umbra that developed around human communities.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-26T19:04:49-08:00","id":1437183,"title":"","content":"<b>Falcon</b> is next, and we're told is the totem of \u201cvictory, freedom, and insight\u201d and hates the Wyrm most of all totems. The freedom part comes from members of the spirit brood Falcon liberated from the Wyrm, but is worded in such a way that implies Falcon himself was once enslaved. That is probably unintended His brood are strong warriors so the Garou have always respected the spirit and his brood. We're also told that great speed, power, and knowledge are part of his spirit realm, and ties to Helios are mentioned. The main description is disjointed with one thing added after another. It lacks a focus that could better tie everything together. Something like \u201cFalcon is the kind of the birds of prey, and his keen eye is like the sun overlooking the land, searching for his enemy the Wyrm.\u201d Then build on your thesis.<br /> <br /> We're only given <i>three</i> spirits. They're evocative, but very limited. The Talons of Horus are a tremendous flock of falcon spirits \u2013 but they're rarely seen and the Great Flock left for the deep umbra and hasn't come back since the 14th century. The Children of Karnak are spirit guardians of the temples of Helios. Firebirds are order oriented solar spirits of the Deep Umbra that keep Falcon's lore. These are more like story hooks than spirits one would normally interact with.<br /> <br /> Garou in most chronicles are very unlikely to run into any of them. Again we don't get a real sense of what Falcon's brood comprises of. We're not even told birds of prey are part of his domain! That should be obvious, but still make it explicit. The tribebooks give us a lot of such spirits as totems. And we should have other kinds of spirits appropriate for Falcon's several roles. The three spirits are fine as is, but massively incomplete in the sense of giving tools for an ST to use in an ordinary game. There is a lot of text for these three spirits. Less text and more spirits would have improved things. I mark this as Incomplete.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-27T09:15:16-08:00","id":1437242,"title":"","content":"This is really impressive, I love what you're doing here Black Fox. I just wanted to add some of my thoughts about Falcon's potential brood, just to show my thinking I'm basing some of them on the supposed qualities of his chosen tribe the Silver Fangs (Nobility, Purity) as well as those spirits I think touch on birds of prey or solar archetypes. <br /> <br /> First off I want to add Swan. It's a symbol of purity and nobility, it's also just a big vicious bird which I think would fit in with Falcon's theme. Also, there are a lot of stories that connect swan with imprisonment such as the swan princess or the Sister with seven brothers both of which deal with people trapped in the form of swans, so falcon could have freed a trapped swan spirit. I think there was some connection between Bunyip and blackswan so there might be some tensions there but don't quote me on that. Kestrel as a smaller bird of prey would also fit in well with Falcon, I'm not sure about others some like Eagle or Kite I feel a bit hesitant to link them as Eagle feels like it should have it's own brood while the reputation of Kite's tends to be less noble than Falcon. Maybe Kite could be the slightly darker more pragmatic member of the spirit brood the doer of evil so Falcon can stay true and good.<br /> <br /> I also think you could make a case for certain spirits of Helios brood to also be in Falcon's brood maybe some that are linked to the dawn, or illumination. I also think that certain air elementals should be in this brood, Spirits of swift breezes or thermals are an obvious addition as birds of prey use them to help them hunt.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26270-undeadpuppy","name":"Undeadpuppy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-27T10:33:29-08:00","id":1437246,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of Swan - it makes a lot of sense for the reasons you've given. Thumbs up.<br /> <br /> I also like your addition of &quot;purity&quot; to Falcon. One issue with the Silver Fangs is their need for a minimum amount of Pure Breed, and whether that is merely a cultural thing or part of the nature of its totem. By incorporating spirits associated with &quot;purity&quot; in Falcon's brood, we build a case for that spiritual component. <br /> <br /> And it just sparked an idea. Werewolf has a Raven legend where the bird was once white, but his coat turned to black when it got too close to Helios. Falcon is also associated with Helios, but he did not have a similar story of his coat of feathers changing color. Unlike Raven, Falcon did not become &quot;burned&quot; or tarnished, soiled, discolored, etc. In terms of myth, this is like saying Falcon retained his purity - perhaps as a blessing by Helios or reflecting Falcon's essence as a spirit of respect/honor. So that would make a great explanation why Silver Fangs need a minimal amount of Pure Breed to be accepted by Falcon - because he is a symbol of Purity and his children must reflect that. Also creates some interesting conundrums in regards to Metis, but myths need not be consistent or even coherent. And there's certain things an ST might do - for example maybe the only Metis kept by the tribe are those that appear &quot;normal&quot;, but any Metis showing too obvious deformity is not inducted into the tribe. There's a lot of ways to handle that, and people's tastes will vary.<br /> <br /> It'll be a while before I get to Bunyip and/or Rainbow Serpent (the Bunyip's tribal totem was retroactively changed between Rage Across Australia and W20). But I think that brood will be mostly limited to all the unique animals of the Australian continent. As you say, black swan could fit there or be under Falcon. A lot of this is personal taste as there aren't clear distinctions, and some broods have spirits that are very similar. But there's no problem for broods to be in conflict or rivalrous. In fact, it adds a lot of flavor and helps an ST in roleplaying spirits differently so that the umbra can be more interesting.<br /> <br /> I agree that more solar spirits could be tied to Falcon. All three of those listed are technically a spirit associated with Helios. But as we'll see there is a Dawn spirit, and it belongs in another brood. A wind spirit of thermals that lift up birds is a great idea.<br /> <br /> I hope you can continue to contribute any ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-27T22:06:39-08:00","id":1437326,"title":"","content":"The description of <b>Fenris</b> is a harsh and unforgiving master demanding endless sacrifice, and his brood does the same. None will associate with cowards, but Fenris offers rewards \u2013 bounty and wealth \u2013 for those with courage.<br /> <br /> We're given eight spirits! This is more like it. Unfortunately, the spirits are mostly defined as anything associated with Norse myth. Which is fine, but human culture \u2013 especially a specific historical one \u2013 should not define the Garou. Fenris and his brood should be more than just \u201cNorse myth\u201d. These spirits aren't bad and cover a lot of ground. But it misses the opportunity to think of Fenris as part of a Gaian mythology and not just a Norse one. For example, Fenris is probably best known for biting off the hand of Heimdall for violating an oath, which has the opportunity for some kind of oath keeping spirit. But it's not found here. One thing I do like is the strange inclusion of Cuckoo bird spirits \u2013 simply because as their numbers dwindled the spirits choose Fenris as the totem to protect them and in exchange offered to sing his praises. That's an interesting addition although the justification could have been used for almost any totem. We have spirits for animals, weather, elementals, legendary creatures, and joy. Except for the Norns, which are probably incarna, the spirits are likely all gafflings despite being named for specific gods in Norse myth.<br /> <br /> This is a decent brood with many members, and I can see that many of these spirits have a chance of appearing in game. They're useful for Fenrir Theurges, and might even be found as general spirits in the Penumbra if the ST wants to use them. And you can even file off some of the serial # and use them as other kind of spirits found elsewhere. The key to roleplaying them is to present them as harsh taskmasters preparing a tribe for brutal war, but also presenting rewards to good and loyal service to Fenris. It's still not great as it is a trifle generic distinguished by their obvious Norse inspirations, but this is the first brood we're given that seems \u201ccomplete\u201d as opposed to just a few spirit ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 03-01-2021, 02:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-02-28T23:32:14-08:00","id":1437434,"title":"","content":"Next is the brood of <b>Grandfather Thunder</b>. Thunder is about power and subjugating those beneath you. It is also about subterfuge \u2013 likely derived from thunderclouds hiding the sun and covering the earth in darkness. And that's all. Weather is strangely absent.<br /> <br /> There's only four spirits, but they are ones that should get a lot of use in many chronicles. They're a good lot with variety. They all appear to be gafflings. And they're all examples of spirits of other broods that were subjugated by Thunder \u2013 stormcrows, rogue raven gafflings, night spirits, and pain spirits.<br /> <br /> Where are the thunder, lightning, and rain spirits? Where is the sky father who lords over the clouds? Or spirits of law and order representing his dominion and judgment? What other sacred animals to him exist? Do the Wind Incarna serve him? What about lightning as a source for fire and wildfires? Or a patron to sailing ships? In many mythologies, the storm god is the one who fights or kill the dragon of chaos \u2013 are there spirits reflecting that \u2013 slayers of Wyrm minions?<br /> <br /> This is an example of someone trying so hard to hit a homerun, that they forget about the need for singles and doubles to move people on the bases. There is some good stuff here, but the ST will need to add a lot more to develop Thunder's brood. I like all the spirits here a lot \u2013 it's just another incomplete brood, though one I think can be used a lot.<br /> <br /> In the description of Stormcrow, we're told that Crow was once a powerful Incarna in his own right during the Impergium, but afterwards choose to serve Thunder. I think that would be a good idea fine in isolation, but it overcomplicates things when we try to build additional broods. It makes more sense for Crow to be under an independent Raven as a fellow corvid. (I believe Raven should be its own totem with its own brood, and will make a post later after I get through Axis Mundi. But of course, various Raven spirits appear in lots of broods. But I wouldn't argue the point in someone else's chronicle.) Of course, we have multiple Crow totems \u2013 one listed in Shadow Lords tribebook and another in Bone Gnawers. So perhaps the aspect of Crow for war belongs to Thunder's broods (and cunning in Rat's brood), but another aspect can remain in Raven.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-01T21:29:13-08:00","id":1437572,"title":"","content":"<b>Griffin</b>'s brood are spirits that are independent and capable, bonded together by mutual respect but never coddled by Griffin. They can be divided in three parts \u2013 ancient hunters, modern predators, and the last group a varied collection of spirits that choose to follow Griffin \u2013 aspects of him, scavengers that follow the hunt, and extinct species seeking his protection to not fall into Slumber or fading away entirely. It's an alright description, but I would prefer a more \u201cnature red in tooth and claw\u201d focus.<br /> <br /> There are six spirits. Sabertooth is either an incarna or jaggling depending on low powered he's gotten. Same with Lion, the White Howler totem. Simurgh and Sphinx are likely the same, though as a wisdom aspect of the totem. They could be both a lesser incarna and a class of jaggling servants The we have mammoth gafflings and scavengers. The Scavenger spirits are exceedingly generic, and are probably a class of spirits derived from more specific spirits of actual scavenger creatures that now represent the act of scavenging than actual animals. But they make for a nice variety.<br /> <br /> This is another very incomplete brood that raises a lot of questions. At the time, it probably did a lot to expand Griffin as a totem, but the brood itself seems very incomplete. Besides spirits of extinct animals and legendary hybrids similar to Griffin, we don't have much. This seems like another case where the approach was to throw a bunch of separate concepts at the brood instead of picking a distinct focus and tying spirits to that theme. <br /> <br /> I'd emphasize the idea of hunting and \u201cpredator kings\u201d. Have spirits that represent the instinct or urge to hunt, a kind of spirit of Rage. Spirits of solitary ambush hunting, or pack tactics. What about non-extinct animals? Are any part of Griffin's brood? Or is he an abstract lord of the hunt? Griffin can work as a more abstract figure dealing with celestial messengers, a protector of designated sacred spaces or taboos, an alternate concept of kingship based on it being a hybrid of eagles and lions \u2013 the respective kingly animals of birds and beasts. Those are part of real world lore about griffins. But we need more spirits to fill that out, as well as some kind of thematic unity that is the core of Griffin. We can have ancillary concepts, but it's best is if there is a solid core. Like \u201cguardian of the world as it was before Man.\u201d","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-02T07:09:20-08:00","id":1437628,"title":"","content":"I feel that a lot of the spirits I'd want to give to Griffin already have their own Fera breeds and I'm not sure how they would interact with a werewolf totem. That said I do think that maybe a lesser aspect of Leopard and other spirits of endangered predatory animals would fit in well with his brood. I was also thinking about Griffin's hatred of humanity and how that might fit in with the brood, would he bring in specific man killers or disaster spirits, I feel like some interesting additions might be Mosquito- due to its killing of so many humans, it could be an interesting if unexpected member. I also feel that a spirit of Blackouts or power loss might be good, something that takes away humans technology and returns them to just being apes in a cave being hunted by wolves. I do agree that Griffin's brood feels like it needs to be focused around something.<br /> <br /> With Grandfather Thunder I agree that he could use more weather spirits maybe even giving him certain types of water spirits something like the rusalki or other drowning spirits. From there it's not a big jump to spirits of floods and other natural disasters. When I think of the after-effects of thunder I think of how it panics my dog, So I feel like him having spirits of fear and panic would be a good fit for him, they could even work with his subjugating aspect by imprisoning people with fear.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26270-undeadpuppy","name":"Undeadpuppy","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-02T11:00:06-08:00","id":1437656,"title":"","content":"I agree with your observations. A lot of spirits that would make sense for Griffin make better sense to be a in a brood elsewhere. If the only spirit broods were the Garou totems, it wouldn't be an issue. But I think it makes sense there are other spirit broods - it's the entire point of this thread!<br /> <br /> The other legendary creatures have the same issue, but their specialized nature at least gives us an idea of where to go. Griffin is rough because the most obvious direction can't be used.<br /> <br /> I personally would not go with Mosquito. That shows up later under Wendigo (though I think it's OK to split Mosquito into those that dwell in the northern tundra and those that dwell in the tropics), and similar spirits often appear in the same broods. However, I would not include it because while it kills humans, it is not a predator. It is a disease carrier, and I don't think associating Griffin with disease is the way to go.<br /> <br /> One possibility is to acknowledge that the standard animal gafflings don't go under Griffin, but some other brood. But to create unique jagglings who have choosen to leave their &quot;home&quot; brood in order to serve Griffin. So &quot;lion spirits&quot; don't serve Griffin, but &quot;the Nemean&quot; as a unique jaggling might. And the Nemean could create a very small brood of gafflings to serve him. These jagglings, of course, would all represent the famous specific man killing animals of legend like the Nemean Lion, and the rare cases when predator animals begin to target and kill men (like the Lions of Tsavo). The names for these spirits are likely given by homids/humans to them. After all, why would Griffin and his brood resort to human speech? So we could have icons like &quot;Shere Khan&quot; even though that is a fictional animal because that is what homids might name a dangerous archpredator Tiger spirit jaggling that serves Griffin (or maybe this is the manticore spirit).<br /> <br /> I think some association with the unpredictable and dangerous side of nature to Griffin is good, but I don't think Griffin is a spirit of natural disasters or the Wyld. So they'd need to be carefully crafted. So I think Blackouts might be too general. But a specific kind of event that causes Blackouts would be fine. For example, there are lots of animals that cause power outages - squirrels cause an unbelievable amount of them. So again we don't have &quot;squirrels&quot; as a gaffling animal spirit under Griffin. But we might have a category of spirits &quot;the Wire Chewers&quot; who cause blackouts, and they serve Griffin. Weather related events would be much more impressive, but I would associate those more with Grandfather Thunder or Wendigo or other weather related spirit. But maybe some general causation and vague Wyld spirit who does could have been captured or created by Griffin in the same way that Cockroach uses triatic spirits to build its domain.<br /> <br /> I think I should try to find old episodes of Life After People and look for ideas.<br /> <br /> Animals that were domesticated and have now gone feral could also fit under Griffin. Maybe not the ones that obviously fit under Rat (we'll get to that soon) or another totem, but Mustangs and Wild Pigs (Razorbacks) would certainly fit. We might want to avoid those that create too many ecosystem problems (like feral cats wiping out so many species), but ones that seem to fit into the wild.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A spirit of floods caused by sudden downpour for Grandfather Thunder is a great idea. And various thunder/lightning spirits responsible for different effects would also be an interesting choice (sacring animals like your dog; starting wildfires; etc.). And animals often seem to know when a bad weather event might happen, so maybe a spirit from Thunder that warns such animals (but is invisible to homids and won't communicate with them) I think fits those lines.<br /> <br /> <br /> All good ideas to explore. We can use more brainstorming like this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 03-15-2021, 02:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-02T20:28:57-08:00","id":1437743,"title":"","content":"<b>Owl</b> is described as having a relatively small brood. He finds few spirits worthy of his protection. But he is a totem of wisdom, secret knowledge, and knowledge beyond the ken of others as well as secret spirits he hides from others. It's a potent description \u2013 tight and focused.<br /> <br /> Only three spirits are given, but all very evocative. They all have memorable names and concepts. Probably my favorite spirit in all of Axis Mundi is the Twice Born \u2013 mouse spirits eaten by Owl that dwelled in death and thus learned its secrets, and then regurgitated alive in rebirth. Common before the Gauntlet, they are quite rare now. Death's Breaths are jagglings created when spirits themselves are destroyed and aren't returned to Gaia and are the liminal spirits of memories and knowledge lost upon death. Darklings are spirits that Owl created to help teach the wisdom associated with grief, death, pain, and anguish so these negative things don't overwhelm and destroy them.<br /> <br /> This is a great and creative imagination of Owl's brood. And I can see these showing up in many chronicles at some point. I am not that creative and would just recite a bunch of owls and other nocturnal animals. I want to be able to create spirits that are appropriate, creative, even poetic. The problem is that with just three of them, it's insufficient to see everything that could comprise that brood. <br /> <br /> I would say that Owl is he who sees in darkness. Everything else springs from there. He is the lord of all nocturnal birds. The overlord of all nocturnal predators, and overseer of all nocturnal animals. He is at home with silence and darkness, and the lord of the woods at night. He teaches the unknowable mysteries unseen by others. And Owl has superb hearing and sight. So there should be spirits of darkness, silence, vigilance, bringers of unwanted but necessary knowledge, and a scattering of other nocturnal animals that have arguable ties to Owl. If we got five more spirits at the same level of the three we have, the brood would be awesome.<br /> <br /> This is much better than the totems in the Silent Striders tribebook which is just a list of animals associated with Egypt plus the celestial objects North Star and Sirius which are important because of their aide to determine place and time, important to travelers. Unlike most tribebook totems and Axis Mundi spirits, there is very little that gels together. Most of those totems seem better suited for other broods. And that's fine since not all friendly spirits need to belong to the same brood. The spirits here soar above the depiction of spirits in the tribebooks.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-03T21:07:03-08:00","id":1437912,"title":"","content":"<b>Pegasus</b> is next. We're not really given a description of the totem or what Pegasus represents. Just a complaint that man treats woman terribly, and then an attempt to reconcile the Greek myth of Pegasus with Garou lore. But it doesn't really work. Since the Furies are said to be one of the oldest tribes, Pegasus must predate the Greek myth of Bellerophon by thousands of years. This is always something the game has struggled with. The obvious links at face value - <i>Black Furies = Amazons = Greek Myths = Greeks</i> - falls apart upon inspection. The Amazons were not Greeks, but barbarians to the Greeks. And there is nothing particularly feminine about the Greek myth of Pegasus. And the chimera monster of Greek myth does not fit the actual Chimera of Garou totems. Various attempts to explain and reconcile this has never been satisfactory in my opinion.<br /> <br /> However, the spirits described are better and form a unity we're not given in the description. Again we only have four spirits. The chimera of legend is now the <i>Chima<u>e</u>ra</i> gaffling, created by Pegasus to represent man's divided nature and allow him to slay it, becoming a better person and thus capable of treating women better. It's a very psychological spirit. But it is rarely summoned because one needs a dragon's blood, hair, or excrement to summon it! Needless to say, it's almost never summoned. It's a Gaffling, but only one exists. It's a noble attempt to incorporate the real myth in a meaningful way for Garou, but doesn't quite work. What purpose does an almost impossible to summon unique Gaffling have in the game? This came so close just to fail. I think a second or third rewrite may turn it into something useful in game.<br /> <br /> We have a Pegasus gaffling, which is obvious but great to have as we now have details on what Pegasus is supposed to be like. They only exist to perform brief missions for Pegasus \u2013 to save someone from danger or provide warning or wisdom, or announce omen of sorrow. Then we build on earlier game concepts and it states Pegasus will never appear in from of a Get of Fenris except to attack one! It's solid.<br /> <br /> We also get the obvious neopagan concept of the Triple Goddess as the Triptych spirit. And then the Haima, the Red Mothers, which is essentially the spirit of menstruation. However, the Haima is done very well, and it's the best spirit out of the bunch. Not sure how much practical use it'll get, but very fitting for a game containing a Black Fury.<br /> <br /> The common theme of these spirits, of course, is \u201cwoman\u201d and \u201cPegasus as a protector of women\u201d. Build on that for the totem's brood. The four spirits here (except maybe Chimaera because it ultimately doesn't do the job it's supposed to do) are actually a good foundation. It just needs to be expanded upon, touching on the sacred feminine, women as an embodiment of it, and the need to protect it.<br /> <br /> I think the issue is to get past the Greek myths and figure out a purely Garou interpretation of Pegasus that still reconciles Greek Myths. I think the first step is to stop thinking of the totem as \u201cPegasus\u201d since it is so loaded as a term, and think how the same symbol might be depicted earlier.<br /> <br /> Pegasus basically is a white winged horse so let's think of it as that. White, like silver, is the lunar color. As a winged creature, Pegasus is an intermediary from the heavens to the earth, representing a divine messenger. She brings the wisdom of the moon, and the moon is a potent symbol of women because its cycle mirrors the menstrual cycle. In the time when the Black Furies formed, the horse was an untamed animal. It would not become domesticated until around 5000-3000 BC which is probably after the Impergium ends. So at the time, it might have been a symbol of the Wyld. And white horses in general show up in many different mythologies representing an entity of exceptional powers, divinity, or excellence. So we can say the White Winged Horse (aka Pegasus) represents a carrier or bearer of the power of the sacred feminine from Luna to earth. The Furies wanted Artemis as a totem, but she sent Pegasus instead. I think it is for the same reason Father Wolf is not the totem of any tribe either \u2013 Luna does not pick favorites, all Garou and Changing Breeds are her nieces and nephews. Pegasus was the consolation prize.<br /> <br /> (That still doesn't explain why Pegasus hates Fenris and his Get so much. Metagame reasons is that originally the Get were positioned as the tribe that discriminated against women. When that was eventually jettisoned, the ban of Pegasus stayed the same. Interestingly, this was always one way. Pegasus would never accept a Get of Fenris as a pack member, but Fenris had no problem with a Black Fury being one of his children We likely need to invent a reason why Fenris as a totem angered Pegasus as a totem. Maybe he tried to eat him.)<br /> <br /> So we need spirits that represent or confer the spiritual gifts of femininity, especially the ability to give birth to life. We also need spirits that protect those gifts and the bearers of them. And certain spirits associated with the moon. The four spirits of Axis Mundi are a perfect foundation for this \u2013 we just need more examples. The Tribebook again is disappointing \u2013 relying mainly on Greek myths instead of universalizing the theme of the totem. I think things associated with traditional, universal women's roles would be a good start \u2013 weaving, sewing, and other work that can be done by mothers who need to stay close to toddlers. It's stereotypical, but I think that's only bad if that is <i>all</i> you do. Those archetypes should come into play. We just should not be limited to them and also look at non-traditional roles, and other archetypes that center on women.<br /> <br /> Amazons of course are an important inspiration. So I would assign Aray from the Players Guide to Garou here. Aray \u2013 obviously derived from Ares \u2013 is supposed to be an early war spirit. I'm still not happy about spirits derived from human culture for Garou, but unlike other war gods, Ares was always more a manifestation of brutality, bloodlust, and the savagery of combat than a true personality. It's a very primordial concept, and Aray should be more like that. More like a force of the Wyld similar to the frenzying maenads. And of course, the legendary Amazons are said to be the daughters of Ares which creates a tie if we believe the Furies had Amazons as their kinfolk (or were the legendary Amazons themselves). <br /> <br /> Ares was supposed to have mated with the naiad Harmonia. Naiads were nymphs of springs, brooks, wells, and fountains. So there's a possibility we might place Sulis \u2013 a spirit of thermal springs from the Players Guide to Garou \u2013 here. A freshwater nymph represents the giving of life, and therefore embodies a feminine concept. I think Sulis could be in several broods (I mentioned earlier it could be in Bear's), but a strong argument could be made here. Or if &quot;Sulis&quot; is in Bear then &quot;Harmonia&quot; a similar spirit could be here.<br /> <br /> Likewise you could place various equine spirits here. Not only are they tied to Pegasus, but the legendary Amazons were masters of horse riding. That conflicts with an earlier idea that feral horses are under Griffin. Most horses now are domesticated horses which conflicts with the Furies as being heavily tied to the Wyld. So this may only be a historical association than does not exist in modern games except for the very few wild horses reintroduced in Mongolia (the last tarpan died in 1909). There's also zebras, an equine animal that has never been domesticated. Few Garou have connections to sub-Saharan Africa, and it'd be a distinguishing mark. Besides, outside of the Asia, the Greeks identified other Amazon tribes in Africa (albeit north of the Sahara as they had very limited information farther south). There's also a role for some kind of vengeful punishment spirit. Pegasus's tribe is named after the Furies after all. And in many myths Artemis punishes lots of men for defiling her temples, sacred places, or attempting to violate her priestesses. So some kind of Nemesis spirit would be good. And as an essentially lunar spirit, perhaps some variant of a Lune.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-04T21:20:25-08:00","id":1438070,"title":"","content":"<b>Rat</b> is defined as the wild incarnate in a survive or die environment, and specifically towards the \u201curban wylderness\u201d and that rat's power comes from his great numbers. This is a good theme for a brood, but I'm a disappointed no thought has been given to Rat before the rise of modern cities or human civilization. It's quick reproduction, large litter size, and role as small scavenger would have had some merit for the omega tribe when civilization was fairly small.<br /> <br /> We're given 7 spirits here which makes Rat one of the better defined broods in the book. These are mainly gafflings of animal and other spirits one would expect in the city. The only real surprise is a Wanderlust spirit for those Bone Gnawers on the move. There's rat and raccoon gafflings. While I understand raccoons are now a common animal in some cities, I don't think it really fits within Rat's brood. Heap Gafflings represent trash spirits, but were originally the spirits that represented things that could be scavenged. It's a neat little spirit. We also learn that these are &quot;deserters&quot; of the Great Trash Heap which is now an Incarna in its own right. I like this a lot. Urban Englings are just englings in a more appropriate form for the urban penumbra. Then there are Feral Psychagonea and Lost Dogs (the spirits of abused animals and feral pets).<br /> <br /> Overall this is a solid, but unspectacular brood. It has some interesting new spirits, but nothing incredible like we've seen in some of the past broods. It covers the basics and has lots of spirits any urban based chronicle will run into again and again. Bone Gnawers are seen as a mostly urban tribe, but I would have liked to see examples of spirits from an earlier time when their defining trait would the \u201comega tribe\u201d in a wilderness setting. Since both Cockroach and Rat covers &quot;cities&quot; it would have been nice to see a clear distinction between what kind of spirits fall into either, but there is a loose division between the more Weaver spirits go into Cockroach and the more Wyld spirits go here. Still &quot;Scab Birds&quot; could have gone here just as well as Cockroach. Still this is a good brood which will get lots of use in any urban chronicle. I think this is a good example of what some of the other broods might have been liked if they added another four spirits to their list of 3-4.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-05T21:12:20-08:00","id":1438244,"title":"","content":"<b>Stag</b> is said to have a large and unified brood, protecting it well as befits a totem of respect and honor. He wants his children to try to solve their own problems before calling him, but he will do it when needed. My perceptions of Stag is heavily influenced by Bambi so I expect him to be the Prince of the Forest and represent the bounty of the land. That's not mentioned in the description, so let's see how the spirits compare to my ideal.<br /> <br /> We just hit the motherlode with no less than <i>12</i> spirits for this brood. The variety and scale makes this the best brood in the book hands down despite none of them being at the creative level of, say, the Twice-Born of Owl. I wish the other broods had been done this well. Good job to Harry Heckel, the writer per the credits. First, we get a wide variety of spirits representing dawn, grain alcohol, antelopes, rabbits, brooks, squirrels, kelpies. Second, we get different power levels with lots of gafflings, then jagglings like Black Stags and White Harts, and either unique jagglings or lesser incarna such as Dawn, Hind, and Yale.<br /> <br /> We don't get anything really imaginative, but the number and variety make up for it. (I use Stag's brood as my goal for my own attempts at defining broods.) In an ideal world, all of those broods would have development like this plus some imaginative spirits at level where we find in Owl and Chimera or even Pegasus. The spirits of Stag's brood should get a lot of play in most wilderness based chronicles. Their large numbers and unity/loyalty to Stag are a good way to distinguish them from other broods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 03-06-2021, 01:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-06T22:25:12-08:00","id":1438435,"title":"","content":"Next is <b>Uktena</b> which is linked to water, search for hidden knowledge, and lots of snakelike creatures, including those from other cultures. It's fairly basic, but it is a strong, resonant theme.<br /> <br /> We get six spirits. Four of which are large serpent monsters from different cultures, and two being fish and snake gafflings given silly names (Dakwa's Children and Inadu). Somewhat boring, but it gets the job done. The writer defined his totem brood and then produced spirits that fit it. We can do lots of variations of a theme, but the main things are covered. We could do one or two truly creative spirits, but the spirts here will get lots of play \u2013 unlike say Chimera's or Falcon's. It's a solid C+.<br /> <br /> It fits a needed role though compared to broods I've mentioned earlier. Uktena is basically a spirit of the fresh waters and legendary serpents representing hidden knowledge and the obstacles to overcome them. That separates it from more saltwater creatures or other reptiles found elsewhere. Not all spirits will easily fall into Uktena versus Dragon Kings or Turtle or Old Man Sea, but most will and you wing the rest.<br /> <br /> The Werewolf totem Uktena is actually a combination of two separate spirits/creatures of Indian myth and legend. There are various horned serpents (known by many names, but the Cherokee calls it Uktena) and the underwater panther of the Great Lakes peoples, usually called Mishipeshu or some variant. In fact while the name Uktena is used, most visual depictions of the spirit in the game better matches Mishipeshu. Both have similar features. Both seem to represent the dangers of the waters \u2013 especially rapids and the like. Both can cause storms, and both are opponents of Thunderbird. Sometimes they are viewed as helpful and protective creatures, but more often they are seen as dangerous and malevolent. For Werewolf, we can consider that to be the difference from human perspective versus that of the Garou. I'll just call this combined creature Uktena for ease.<br /> <br /> So we might add spirits of whitewater rapids, flash floods, whirlpools, and other freshwater spirits that could be considered to be dangerous as well as any water that originate from below the ground (as opposed to falling from the heavens) so it can be a spirit of wells, geysers, and reservoirs. Besides the fish who live here, we might add some freshwater aquatic birds as well. Some of these bird spirits might be associated in other broods, but this could be another place to those that are not obviously part of <br /> <br /> I think it's also appropriate to add some sort of Healing or Medicine spirits. That is also something that fits legend, and it ties in well to various healing snakes in the Old World. We could call them Asclepians. This might be controversial to some people who want Uktena and Wendigo to be merely North American based. But I've come to the conclusion that these spirits and their broods should have been known to the Old World Garou \u2013 though perhaps known by other names. Likewise, spirits of poisons and venoms are also located here.<br /> <br /> These kind of water spirits were said to live under the earth, so we could extend correspondences to Uktena like caves and subterranean passages. Since these are often created by underwater water erosion, it makes sense. So any spirits associated with cave networks could potentially fit here.<br /> <br /> These water spirits often had special attendants that served as lookouts for their enemy Thunderbird. They would appear to let Uktena know whether there were clouds about. There were three main sorts - beaver, elk, and deer. Beaver is obviously associated with water, and the last two had horns like Uktena. Theoretically any animal that had attributes linking it to Uktena could be used. In Werewolf, some of these animals are already claimed by other totems, but we could still create a spirit type, \u201cUktena's Attendants\u201d or &quot;Uktena's Scouts&quot; to represent the spirit that does this role.<br /> <br /> The importance of Uktena's conflict with Thunderbird may have direct relevance for us. Thunderbird is clearly a spirit and lord of thunder and storms. So what is the connection between Thunderbird and Grandfather Thunder? Personally, I would say that while there is a separate Thunderbird spirits and pack totems, that ultimately \u201cthe\u201d Thunderbird is just a different name for Grandfather Thunder. There is no need for two separate spirits that control the skies, weather, storms, and thunder. It also fits the theme that the thunder god is opposed to a serpent figure which is found throughout the Old World. So we can use this legend to explain a conflict between broods of Thunder and Uktena. This actually fits well with the stories of the Shadow Lords being very hostile to the tribe. It could also explain why we see Thunderbird mentioned among Wendigo and even Pumonca, but not with Uktena.<br /> <br /> I don't think <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/21481-onkwe\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar21481_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"21481\" data-vbnamecard=\"21481\">Onkwe</a> has posted on this forum recently which is too bad. He'd probably have a lot of insight to add here.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-07T20:16:13-08:00","id":1438519,"title":"","content":"Last we have <b>Wendigo</b>. He is a greatly feared totem with bitter rage born from a cold heart. I take that to mean he has no mercy and is unmoved by tears. We're given a story about how Wendigo was once Sasquatch until bad things happened. I never liked that element to the totem and ignore it myself, but it's a burden I have to deal with. And that's it. It's not a good description. It's a partial narrative that is incomplete, and does not bode well. The best totems and broods have strong thematic elements. We'd need something like \u201cWendigo is the spirit of terrible winters that lead to deprivation and possible starvation,\u201d and then build on that strong theme. Obviously there is some of this here, but it is more incidental than thought through.<br /> <br /> I believe that all the totems should have been known to the Garou. So although Wendigo is very culturally specific, I think the Old World Garou just would have known him by a different name. Maybe Fimbulwinter, or the Great Winter, or Boreal Wind as I mentioned before.<br /> <br /> One nice bit is that all members of Wendigo brood can be talked with anyone who knows the Gift Spirit Speech OR the level two Wendigo tribal Gift Speak with the Wind Spirits. <u>This is a great idea.</u> Not only does it improve a rather lackluster Gift, it implies a much greater bond between the Wendigo tribe and Wendigo's brood as potentially all tribe members can speak with their allied spirits, not just theurges. This is an interesting take on the tribe, and a great use of the spirit brood idea. It's very distinctive. The writer gets extra points for this. Incidentally, this is an example of so many great things about the setting that are buried in various sourcebooks, but never get repeated elsewhere. (At least, I don't remember it. Does anyone know if this is mentioned in any of the Wendigo tribebooks?)<br /> <br /> We have five spirits which isn't many, but certainly is better than some of broods. We start with Wolverine which is great. He's known for his ferocious appetite. However, the description describes him as more like a trickster, and emphasizes he is not the pack totem in the Players Guide. This is technically Kwakwadjec (an alternative spelling of Carcajou, a trickster figure of Innu lore) and not &quot;Wolverine&quot;, so he's like a specialized wolverine spirit. It's good to be expanding the brood, but there's a general tendency by the sourcebooks to make the Wendigo less about a fearsome winter spirit and more about generic American Indian humans. And this is not a good sign.<br /> <br /> Next are Atcen which are \u201cice cannibals\u201d created when humans succumb to cannibalism in winter. They fit thematically. Although they are not compared to \u201cFalse Wendigo\u201d spirits mentioned in Ways of the Wolf, I imagine those might be Wyrm corrupted versions of the Atcen. There are also Ice Elementals which are obvious brood members. Windtooth is a kind of wind spirit whose presence is noticed as frostbite. A little more creative treatment of an elemental/weather spirit. I like it a lot. Then there are mosquito spirits, appropriate as they are found throughout the north \u2013 especially on the tundra \u2013 and \u201ceat\u201d people like cannibals do. Great choice in my opinion. I would separate \u201cthese\u201d mosquito spirits from their tropical members.<br /> <br /> I like these spirits, but feel it was a mistake to not make Wolverine an actual Wolverine. It comes from a desire to incorporate more Indian myth and perpetuates the problem that the Wendigo are best defined by human identities as opposed to the totem. It's fine to include some elements as a trickster and perhaps a companion jaggling, but the brood would be strengthened by simply assigning Wolverine here.<br /> <br /> It's a good start, but we have a very narrow brood and thus quickly boring. We need some variety and increased scope, but appropriate to its theme. Usually at this point I turn to the tribebooks for inspiration. Unfortunately, I have low opinion on the Wendigo books. They are less about a cult devoted to a fierce winter spirit and more about generic Indian stuff. First edition tribebook has Salmon, Otter, Jaybird, Peyote, and Thunderbird. What do they have to do with Wendigo or winter or desperate hunger? There are the Winter Manitous, which are obvious, but still limiting. Second edition is not much better giving us Dolphin, Moose, Caribou, Sturgeon, Rabbit, Goose, Porcupine, and Beaver. Caribou is nice, and I can see a Garou myth where Caribou defects from Stag in order to be better protected by Wendigo so it can survive the harsh northern climes. But the rest have little to do with Wendigo's brood, although all are fine pack totems by themselves that Wendigo or other Garou might want to use. We could take the North Wind from the Wind Incarnas and place him here, but it's probably best to keep all the Winds together.<br /> <br /> The Wendigo have a very strong concept, but it's not been properly developed in my opinion. The spirits here are good, but insufficient. We need to do some thinking. I've never had a problem with the idea of Wendigo being a cannibal spirit, but the Garou being against man-eaters. There's a strong concept of the game of spirit taboos, and that Garou adopt certain taboos in exchange for power. Garou vulnerability to silver, the lunar metal, is best explained as a taboo they accepted in exchange for Luna's blessings. That's just how spirits work. Likewise, as a beneficiaries of Wendigo, a cannibal spirit, the tribe does not partake of meat. I see Wendigo as embodying both cannibalism as a result of a starvation winter AND a punisher of said cannibals for violating natural laws. He doesn't transform those people into monsters \u2013 the False Wendigo spirits from Ways of the Wolf does that. <br /> <br /> In a certain sense, he represents a spirit of judgment against transgressors who put their own hunger, consumption, or greed ahead of their community. We could have a spirit that represents not consuming any more than is needed, which in today's world could act against our entire consumer culture and represent an ethical, simple life. There is probably also a starvation spirit, as well as spirits of \u201cstarvation foods\u201d (foods not typically eaten, but only in times of famine. Those could include plants like cattails, certain wild nuts, dandelion roots, chickweed, certain mushrooms, and other forageable plants. I don't know enough myself to name specifics, but there could be a generic spirit type that represents those plants. One or more evergreen trees could also be one, representing survival in a harsh environment. Spruce might be a good tree to use. Lichens and mosses might go here. Possibly spirits of permafrost, peats, and bogs. Strangely, we may wantr to raid Rage Across Russia and find appropriate spirits there since Russia is obviously associated with a lot of the same climate as Wendigo. We'd just need to rename some spirits to make them more universal than using the &quot;give stats to anything mentioned in Russian folklore&quot; approach there. The aurora borealis would also be a good spirit as it is iconic of the arctic circle. It's even listed in the Wendigo tribebook under Allies, and is the only other spirit listed there besides Great Wendigo himself! We may want to include seals and walruses as iconic arctic animals that northern peoples hunt to survive.<br /> <br /> Both Fenris and Wendigo are harsh masters, but if Fenris is about courage, then Wendigo should be about endurance and survival. I also prefer the idea that Wendigo is not human culturally specific and should be known in some form in the Old World (though not by the Wendigo name, at least not until the Old World and New World Garou met and the old tribes learned that spirit was the patron of the Wendigo tribe).<br /> <br /> If I want to do this totem justice, I think I may have to read a lot more Jack London and study more on taiga and tundra ecosystems. It would be easy to pick every animal that lives in the far north and stick it in Wendigo's brood, but I don't like the idea. Every clime should have spirits of many broods in order to make potential chronicles set there interesting and diverse. And Wendigo's brood may be less likely to appear the more south you go, but as this winter's weather shows, Windtooth might even show up in Texas sometime. I just want something more than just winter weather. Of all the tribal totems, I need to think the most on this one.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-08T07:02:55-08:00","id":1438566,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1410223-additional-spirit-broods#post1410223\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think Coyote is complex because officially the actual spirit who is patron of the Nuwisha is not Coyote the animal per se, but the more universal Trickster.</div> </div> </div> </div>But the breedbook says that Coyote is his first name, the Incarna would just acquire new skins/masks/names as the time moved on. Axis Mundi complements mentioning a story of how he acquire new aspects.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1876-fenixpp","name":"Fenixpp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-08T09:56:23-08:00","id":1438584,"title":"","content":"Thanks for your comment.<br /> <br /> The breedbook, like a lot of sourcebooks, is written in character so it is not 100% reliable. The reader cannot take things at face value, especially when it concerns something like Trickster or Coyote or his children. So everyone will come to their own interpretation especially since there are a lot of other things regarding the spirit world that take interpretation..<br /> <br /> If you ultimately rule that it is actually Coyote, and not a separate Trickster figure, it shouldn't affect anything materially. That brood will still act in a certain way, and Nuwisha will also consider their totem to be Coyote. As for me, I go back and forth. That quote is from my first post over half a year ago, so it is very early in this project and I have come to different stances than I held then. At the time I believed each Changing Breed should have its own Major Incarna, and right now I have several Incarna with two or more Changing Breeds. And trying to balance the various definitions and needs of each brood as I go along force me into certain decisions that contradict earlier ones.<br /> <br /> I do think there is a challenge with Coyote's brood in the sense that there are no Coyotes in the Old World. So its brood and its leader will have to be interpreted as something else until the Old World Garou make contact and know what a Coyote is. While it is possible they might interpret Coyote as a very strange wolf, I think it is much more likely that Coyote/Trickster would appear to them as a figure they would already recognize as a trickster figure. The most obvious choice would be Fox. But I am still uncertain what to do with the Kitsune. But I definitely want some kind of animal so it could be universal rather than a figure from a specific human culture. One reason I started doing existing broods from Axis Mundi was to give me a break, and to define the existing broods, as the next speculative ones are a bit harder to define and need to fit everything else that's been established. So when I am ready to discuss Fox and the Kitsune, I'll likely revisit the issue of Coyote.<br /> <br /> I don't remember any discussion about Coyote in Axis Mundi, but I could easily have glanced over it. Do you know where it is located?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-08T13:02:53-08:00","id":1438606,"title":"","content":"You're welcome ^^.<br /> <br /> The discussion is from old Garou talking around a bonfire, in the topic &quot;Naming&quot; at page 18 from the Axis Mundi. It doesn't state exactly which aspect from him came from where, but it's a reference. I see many times people discarding information due to be from a POV and not from the authors, while this is healthy to study this lore with the most clean lenses possible I believe that each account corroborated by other characters turns the information given more robust and this wonderful cosmology its &quot;hmpf&quot;.<br /> <br /> For exemple: Book of the Wyld says that Gaia is not the Wyld and vice-versa, also says that Gaia can not exist without the Wyld, or the Weaver or the Wyrm, She relies on each Supernal equally (page 24). Gaia is the source of spirituality for the Wyld (page 38). If She can give even the Possibility Itself its spirituality we can assume that the Wyrm and the Weaver also got from Her. In the Hengeyokai book we got Bai-Mianxi seeing Gaia personally and the description is pretty... suspect... I quote (page 129):<br /> <br /> &quot;<span style=\"color:#000000\">There she saw a great wonder - the robe itself moved and changed as she watched - the fabric seemed to be part cobweb, part flame, with a green serpent-dragon writhing between them. The webs trapped and snuffed out the flames; the smoldering edges of spider-silk blazed up again; the serpent wove in and out of the chaos, tearing the cloth with its great teeth and patching it up again with its claws.&quot;</span><br /> <br /> It's a pure POV from Bai-Mianxi, I know, but as it seems, Gaia is all of the Triat when working in unison, cobweb is the Weaver, flames are the Wyld and the dragon is the Wyrm. I don't mean to enforce this view, just showing that this view of mine has some support ehehe.<br /> <br /> IMO Kitsune could have Inari has their Incarna. In Europe the Fox is some of the archetype tricksters, but some spirits can be adopted by other Incarna, it happens as shown by the Lion and the Griffon. Coyote could have some fox spirits in its brood (His Loki aspect in Europe) and Inari has some too, two main broodlines if you will xD. I hope your project goes all well and that my ramblings have helped you somehow.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1876-fenixpp","name":"Fenixpp","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"54"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-09T23:09:32-08:00","id":1438895,"title":"","content":"I found the next few potential broods and major incarna harder to formulate, so before I addressed them I wanted to cover the existing broods. I wanted to be sure I did not repeat myself.<br /> <br /> It's been a good exercise by itself. Examining the broods told me there are some things I neglected when designing the other broods. I only thought of spirits that naturally fell into the major incarna's theme or correspondences. But Axis Mundi showed me that sometimes spirits join a brood because 1) the Totem specifically created them to carry out a needed function, or 2) the spirits choose to follow the Totem for protection (or were subjugated by them). <br /> <br /> While these last two require more creative approach, it does give the ST more flexibility when assigning or creating spirits for that brood. I won't be commenting much on those points in this thread, but I'll remember as I continue to refine the spirit broods I use in my own chronicles.<br /> <br /> So now I'll return to the subject of additional spirit broods.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-09T23:10:53-08:00","id":1438897,"title":"","content":"Large Herd Animals Part I<br /> <br /> I do think there is still a major gap for the spirit broods. That would be large animals that move in herds. Things like buffalo, bison, cattle, elephants, and the like. At one point I was thinking of two separate broods \u2013 one for herd animals (like bison), and one for large animals (like elephants), but the more I studied them I decided these overlapped and are best just one brood. These are generally prey animals.<br /> <br /> Many prey animals fall under Stag. And we also have Boar. But I felt these animals were thematically different. Stag's prey animals, while some are herd animals, stay safe by running away. Boar's prey animals are dangerous and fight back individually (some of these are small enough to be prey for larger animals, but are also predators themselves to smaller animals). These animals though employ a herd defense \u2013 weaker animals inside while the stronger ones defend them \u2013 and those that are taken are the stragglers who don't form up in time. They stand their ground and do not run away. It's not a perfect definition, but a division of \u201cthose who run\u201d, \u201cthose who fight\u201d, and \u201cthose who stand\u201d is good enough to work. STs can easily slot random prey animal there if a spirit comes into play and its brood matters.<br /> <br /> There are several totem spirits that fall into this category. Buffalo in Wild West, and the Players Guides have Bison and Bull. Elephant is mentioned in Uktena Tribebook, probably more because it is \u201cexotic\u201d and an early feature of Uktena was joining with people around the world. Mammoth would fit, but we know it now belongs to Griffin's brood. Before they died out, it might have been here. In fact, it might have even been the major incarna that lead it!<br /> <br /> Although not mentioned as totems, animals like hippos, giraffes, wildebeests, wild yak, and muskox fit here. I also think we should the marine mammals that also fit the idea of large herd animals that eat grass \u2013 manatees and dugongs. It both diversifies this brood as well as adds another layer to the ocean diversity.<br /> <br /> One thing we should discuss is the nature of Bull. Is Bull actually the spirit of domesticated (male) cattle, or is it more like Aurochs, its now extinct wild ancestor? A case can be made for both, but the answer may have consequences. If Bull is actually Aurochs, how does it continue to survive as a spirit? Is it still too soon for it to fade? Might Aurochs be better off under Griffin? Is there a reason Griffin might not accept him (perhaps allowing himself to be \u201cdomesticated\u201d offends Griffin.) If instead Bull is the domesticated animal, should it be elsewhere like some theoretical brood of domesticated animals (this issue will come up again as the game has several domestic animal totems, and I will try to address it in a later post)? Or is \u201cBull\u201d more like the essence of a strong, aggressive animal that transcends its domesticated nature? STs will need to decide this.<br /> <br /> Of course Bull and Aurochs brings up the topic of the Apis, the werebulls. It is probable that Aurochs/Bull might even have been the original leader of the brood as having a Changing Breed would provide it with a lot of spiritual support and power. Apis supposedly oversaw the the cycle of birth, reproduction, and death. These would have been more important to the brood when Aurochs was present, but I believe Aurochs lost rulership of the brood sometime after the destruction of the Apis. When I'm not sure, but after the Aurochs became extinct around 1627 it certainly could not have remained the leader. An import time in the history of this brood might have been when Aurochs lost power and another spirit became the incarna who lead the brood.<br /> <br /> Lastly, Apis are supposed to be the source of the Minotaur legend, and of course the Minotaur spirit is behind the Skindancers \u201ctribe\u201d. Now I don't believe there was any intention at the time to link the Minotaur spirit of the Skindancers to the Apis and Aurochs at all. But this could be a nice synchronicity. Some kind of surviving spirit could have been angry at the Garou and agreed to strike back at them. It's even similar to Bat falling to the Wyrm after the destruction of the Camazotz. More interesting is Minotaur's anger towards Pegasus. I'm not sure of the reason why unless the original writer thought two legendary creatures of Greek myth should be enemies of each other. We might invent a reason why such as the Black Furies were heavily responsible for the destruction of the Apis. While Minotaur would not be a member of this brood, it could indicate a degree of resentment, disappointment or anger of the brood to the Garou.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-09T23:11:40-08:00","id":1438898,"title":"","content":"Large Herd Animals Part II<br /> <br /> At this point, we need to figure out who the major Incarna of this brood is. I think either <b>Elephant or Bison/Buffalo</b> are our two best candidates. Both are endangered animals, though the Bison's long term survival is probably better. I am willing to be convinced of another candidate should someone make an argument. Simply for geographic diversity, having an African animal like Elephant (though they exist in Asia too) as a brood leader would be nice. But Bison has its own advantages. As a fellow bovine, it might have been easier for it to \u201cinherit\u201d the position. This is important because obviously more correspondences will be created once we have the major incarna. Elephant would have correspondences with memory, and ivory which might expand the brood to includes other tusked animals like walruses. Bison might emphasize the importance of being a food source or source of economic benefit.<br /> <br /> One interesting aspect (especially if you consider Mammoth to originally be the brood's leader) is that as endangered animals, the brood is particularly vulnerable as a result of failing leadership. The entire brood seems under assault with so many key animals/spirits being lost over the years. This could have interesting consequences in the Umbra. And it could lead to a very distinctive roleplay among these spirits \u2013 very wary, nervous, untrusting, paranoid, etc. With many spirits who once belonged to this brood falling to slumber, or fading entirely away. This might mean this is one of the weaker and smaller broods. Maybe there is a concerted effort by the Wyrm to destroy this brood entirely. That could lead to some interesting chronicles.<br /> <br /> Interestingly, these are also the animals that mankind has attempted in the past century to retrieve from extinction. There have been historical and current attempts to bring back both Mammoths and Aurochs from extinction, as well as concerted preservation efforts for bison/buffalo, and the African megafauna. Perhaps this could in part be attributed to the work of the packs of Mammoth following its ban.<br /> <br /> So what other kind of spirits could make up this brood? They are known for their size and strength though I can't think of spirits based on those properties along. There is a safety in numbers theme so perhaps there is some kind of protective spirit that gathers/watches the herd much like a shepherd dog. And these animals provide a lot of sustenance (and other economic goods \u2013 hides, ivory, leather, etc.) to nearby people who either domesticated them or actively hunted them. So there may be spirits of bounty and wealth.<br /> <br /> I also think they impact the soil a lot \u2013 their manure and urine fertilize it, their hoofs help bury seeds, and the both dig up the soil and impact it. And these species impact the environment. Their large size and need to browse and graze prevents woodlands from getting too dense. They knock down trees, trample vegetation, eat shrubs and small trees, and open up dense stands of bushes and trees so they can graze the meadows and grasslands. Without them, dense forests can spring up. So they could be seen as a kind of \u201clords of the grasslands\u201d - animals who not just dominate their landscape, but also created it. So perhaps some sort of fertility spirits, some grasses, or even a landscape spirit of the steppe/savanna could be here. There might also be some kind of \u201cbig sky\u201d spirit to reflect the sun able to send light to illuminate the entire land, unlike forests whose canopies can block out of the sun.<br /> <br /> Their large piles of dungs attract a lot of insects, so certain flies and beetles could go here. Large herbivores like these often have inefficient digestion, so their waste provides a lot of nutrients back to the environment. I think it would be easy to simply dump most insects into a small group of Insect totems, so putting coprophagous insects here would provided welcome diversity.<br /> <br /> And these insects in turn provide meals for a lot of grassland insectivores like bats, birds, and smaller ground animals. Meadowlarks, crested larks, and pipits are examples. This might be a good place for some of them to nest here, grateful to the larger animals as the \u201cfounders of the feast\u201d. If Stag's brood includes lots of woodland animals, than this brood would include a lot of similar animals that live in the grasslands instead of forests (for example, maybe ground squirrels belong in this brood, but tree squirrels belong to Stag). Baboons typically live in savannas as well, but raises the question about primates in general, especially the more humanlike monkeys and great apes. Should they belong to their own brood?<br /> <br /> This could be a very diverse and rich brood, but of great importance to Gaia. A distinctive quality that it has a lot of prominent species that have gone extinct under the Garou's watch, and there could be an element of the Garou failing this brood time and again. And it's own that mostly been ignored in Werewolf since the animals that make up its brood aren't typical or preferred totems of players. But I think it could play an important role in a lot of games, and the spirit brood could have a very distinctive attitude in terms of roleplay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-14T14:12:33-07:00","id":1439890,"title":"","content":"As I borrow friends' old copies of the breedbooks, I'm revisiting some of their patrons as major incarna. Per my first post, I originally did not want to combine multiple breeds under one Trickster. However, now that I combined Nagah and Mokole under one totem, and the various Bastet under one as well, I am reconsidering it. I'm not sure if both Fox and Coyote are part of a single Trickster's brood, but am open to it. The real issue is whether it makes sense or is even possible to create two separate spirit broods distinguishable from each other rather than combining them under one. But this post actually isn't about them, but to discuss a third totem often associated with tricksters \u2013 Raven.<br /> <br /> I think it is best to continue to keep <b>Raven</b> as its own brood. Although a trickster figure, I think the game has given Raven enough distinctive qualities that Raven is more than just a trickster, although that is still a role. The Corax have a very different function than tricksters \u2013 they are the messengers, scouts, and informants of Gaia. And they have lots of other distinguishing features. Ravens are carrion birds and Corax therefore have ties to death and the underworld. And in the setting the Corax are tied to Helios. In human mythology and culture, while foxes and coyotes are universally seen as cunning and tricky animals, ravens have a more varied interpretation. So at least for my chronicles, I am putting Raven with its own spirit brood. If you want to put it under a single Trickster brood where all these animals, that can work too.<br /> <br /> I start where I normally do and look at the published pack totems to look at those I think share common themes. As a pack totem, Raven was first published in Ways of the Wolf as a totem of wisdom, and must have been popular enough that it was included in several tibebooks and finally the Revised corebook. There he's associated with wealth (Raven will always provide though the pack must not carry wealth with them) and is interestingly described as the hungriest of totems as well as described as being a trickster. As a totem, he is clearly oriented towards wilderness Garou living off the land, but there's a role for him in urban based packs too. (Interestingly Raven is described as being similar to Coyote, but \u201cnot as powerful\u201d.)<br /> <br /> The main theme as a pack totem is Raven is the provider, a combination of trusting the totem and self-reliance. He lives off the land and so does his children.<br /> <br /> In the same book, we're given Hare who is described as a \u201ctrickster and provider\u201d. Very similar to Raven, so we have an obvious candidate for his brood already. Now the book then states Hare is also known as Mahtigwess of Great Rabbit, this is derived from Native American legend. Of course, while very similar hares and rabbits are not the same, but Werewolf is a game about myth not science, so we'll let it pass. Now a different totem \u2013 Rabbit \u2013 is mentioned in the Wendigo tribebook. So again we have a situation where similar spirits appear in different broods. That's fine. It could be a different spirit, or just that the Wendigo have good relations with the brood of Raven. Either works, but I'll be going with the latter as I don't see anything especially Wendigo-like about rabbits or hares.<br /> <br /> From these two, we're building a theme of tricksters that aren't warriors, with another role as providers. What else might work? There's several candidates in the Players Guide, but I admit my picks are much more speculative and not obvious fits.<br /> <br /> Players Guide to Garou gives us Bacchus. Bacchus is tied to many different tribes. And more importantly for us, he's a \u201conce dead\u201d god with ties to the underworld which fits one of Raven's themes. That fits Owl too, but Bacchus's other roles as an intoxication and fertility figure fits less well there than under Raven At least ravens eat grapes, so a spirit of grape-harvest and wine fits here. Many tribes are listed as being typical children of Bacchus, so it doesn't make sense for him to be part of a spirit brood of only one of the tribal Incarna. A spirit of an independent brood friendly to all Garou makes sense. I really don't like the inclusion of human cultural concepts for Garou (which is a too frequent issue in sourcebooks), but the name Bacchus is clearly a placeholder. I may just name it the Wine Giver, or Fruit of the Vine. Not very happy with those names, so I'll keep working on it.<br /> <br /> Fog is a totem of mystery and secrets which fits raven. Fog spirits seem to show up a lot in Werewolf, so they should go somewhere. A \u201cneutral\u201d brood like Raven makes it available to every tribal Garou equally. But if someone has another suggestion for Fog's placement, I'd love to hear it.<br /> <br /> And while not an obvious pick, I also like Flea. It's a totem of war, but based on guerilla tactics and often seen as cowardly by Garou. So it fits Raven who otherwise would be lacking in war totems. And the totem description says it is just as popular with Red Talons as Bone Gnawers, indicating Flea is best suited with a brood that could appeal to Griffin's children. Raven fits better than the vermin totem of the Urrah tribes. And Fleas like ravens live off the land so to speak, jumping from one hairy body to another. So we're off to a good start.<br /> <br /> Another totem I like is Opposum in Rage Across Appalachia (RAAp). It's a wily creature, and it's trick of playing dead fits Raven in multiple ways (though the totem description strangely makes no reference to it).<br /> <br /> Raven should have a number of birds under it. Corvids and other carrion birds like vultures are appropriate. Having vultures and condors expands the brood quite well. RAAp also has a Woodpecker totem in it, and while there's nothing particularly Raven-like about it, I think it has potential. Woodpeckers aren't trickster figures, but their drilling behavior is unusual and sometimes annoying. And they show a new way of providing for themselves. Unless we put it in a theoretical new brood headed by another bird spirit (such as Wren as I noted in a previous post), it fits better under Raven than with Falcon or Owl.<br /> <br /> That exhausts the published totems for me, but I'm surprised at the number that could fit. I originally thought I might not find any. Another bird species that could work is Shrike, and it is listed as a teacher of one Gift in Corax. It's habit of spearing insects and vertebrates on thorns creates association with death and murder. It could make another appropriate totem of war. With corvids and carrion birds (and possibly woodpeckers) that's a good brood of birds. Birds of prey and seabirds are also known to eat carrion, but fit better under other broods. But we could devise special versions of such spirits that do fit under Raven just as Griffin has a generic Scavengers spirit.<br /> <br /> Like Falcon, Raven has associations with Helios. They lack the solar regalia / monarchical or guardian spects of Helios, but have a lesser role as the sun's heralds and companions. They're responsible for bringing the bounty of the sun \u2013 its heat and light \u2013 to the world. So instead of Lunes, we might have something like Heliades, some of which belong to Raven (though of course not all, most attend Helios or Katanka-Sonnak directly) to act as solar guides in the umbra or bring light. We could it Lightbringer.<br /> <br /> In terms of other kind of spirits outside of animals, there might be spirits associated with theft, puzzles and problem solving, or cooperation to simulate the mental activities of corvids. Spirits of learning and memory. Maybe a spirit of \u201cshiny things\u201d that represent wealth or beauty. There might be a \u201cwar raven\u201d or \u201cbattle crow\u201d spirit to reflect the bird's martial associations with Celtic and Germanic peoples. This would be similar to the Morrigu of the Fianna, but that's another example of a human cultural entity being subsumed by the Garou. We might even want to put the Morrigu here, though allow that Fianna have an especially close relationship with that spirit.<br /> <br /> Spirits dealing with death are also a natural for a carrion bird, but I think it's important to make a distinction with Owl's brood. It's best to not directly overlap. If Owl's brood is concerned with the mysteries and nature of death, then a tack with Raven might be spirits of decay/rot (and perhaps plants and fungi that mimic such like carrion flowers and stinkhorn mushrooms). Or a spirit that reveals information about a dead body, similar to criminal forensics revealing clues about the nature of the death. Or a spirit that can guide you into the Dark Umbra, but not reveal anything about it. And of course spirits that are an omen or harbinger of bad things to come. More practical applications rather than Owl's wisdom and mysteries.<br /> <br /> Another possibility with the brood, is that perhaps Raven's brood gets a lot of information from other broods given the large number of raven spirits found in other broods. Stormcrows, Hrafn, and Wild Ravens might still share some things they know with their old spirit friends. Who knows, that might even apply to the Carrion Bird spirit (from the White Howlers tribebook) which is now part of Whippoorwill's brood. This would be an interesting roleplay aspect to Raven's brood. We might even want to expand that and have every Garou tribe with its own Raven or corvid spirit in its brood. This could be another reason why the Garou spared the Coarx during the War of Rage.<br /> <br /> I would treat Raven's brood as almost a Garou brood in all but name. It's be friendly to them, just not sponsoring a Garou tribe. But that friendship has its price - Garou are expected to be friendly back to the Corax. And these spirits would be the immediate spirit helpers to the Corax, so they should include any kind of spirit a Corax must have access to. This might include spirits a Garou might not normally think of - certain kinds of wind spirits, navigating spirits, perhaps even simple tool spirits since corvids are among the few animals that use tools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-21T20:45:25-07:00","id":1440898,"title":"","content":"Australian Spirits Part 1<br /> <br /> I now turn to the Bunyip, and so this post becomes a discussion of the spirit of Australia in general. Besides the issue of the Bunyip's spirit brood, the game made a decision that the Penumbra of Australia is different from the rest of Earth's Penumbra. We also need to deal with all of the unique animals of Australia. Both of these are due to Australia's remote location and isolation from the rest of the continents, and both will have impact on the Bunyip spirit brood.<br /> <br /> First we need to address is who is the Incarna totem of the Bunyip? Originally it was the Rainbow Serpent, but later the game made the decision it was Bunyip itself. I don't know the reason for this change, but it might have been thought inappropriate for such a powerful spirit to be the patron of single tribe, or was possibly done out of some concern for indigenous belief. I don't think it really matters, and while I will go with one interpretation for this thread, I don't think it'll impact things if an ST chooses a different answer to the totem question.<br /> <br /> Personally, I think it works best for Bunyip to be the totem of the tribe, and for Rainbow Serpent to represent a kind of Incarna for Australia in general, recognizing its unique ecosystem as a result of its isolation (which for our purposes might include New Guinea). In the game, we don't really have things like \u201cspirit of this large ecosystem or continent\u201d, but it's the same vein as City Fathers or the Leshii in Rage Across Russia (a \u201cspirit master of the forest\u201d instead of a spirit of an individual tree or plant). Perhaps due to Australia's geographic and biological isolation from the rest of the world, it has a tutelary Incarna or its Incarna is particularly more powerful or important than that of any other landmass.<br /> <br /> The two Incarna are heavily connected as Bunyip is an explicit protector of Rainbow Serpent. For practical purposes, they are the same brood, and for ease of reference I'll simply call it Bunyip's brood. But technically, they should be two broods, with Bunyip's spirit for the Garou tribe, and Rainbow Serpent representing the land itself. But someone putting them together as one is fine too. Rainbow Serpent might not even have its own brood, being content to be a kind of spiritual suzerain over all broods in its lands. Not all powerful spirits need broods at all, or have only a small number of spirits in its brood. In which case, Bunyip's actual spirit brood would incorporate a lot of the spirits we might otherwise assign to Rainbow Serpent. Since the legends of the Rainbow Serpent associate it with waterholes and rivers and the like (it sounds very similar to Uktena in this way), and Bunyip is a creature found in rivers and swamps, there is a natural crossover in themes and concepts. Perhaps technically Bunyip is part of Rainbow Serpent's brood while being patron of a tribe itself.<br /> <br /> (I never liked the idea of were-marsupials being Garou, so in my chronicles they are just one of the Changing Breeds, but one that filled the warrior role in Australia. It doesn't really require any real change in the setting, but seems more right to me. But for those you keep the Bunyip as Garou, it works just as well.)<br /> <br /> I originally thought that Bunyip would likely be the default home of most unique Australian creatures. But rereading Rage Across Australia changed that. Many of the spirits there are hostile towards Garou. But other unique Australian wildlife still serve as totems or can be friendly spirits. Regardless whether those animals belong to some special brood under another Australian spirit, or belong to one of the other broods, they can't be part of Bunyip's brood. As Bunyip's brood is now extremely hostile to Garou.<br /> <br /> But that gives us a good distinguisher. A decision an ST must make in any Australian chronicle is whether this type of spirit is hostile or not. If it is hostile, it belongs to Bunyip's brood. If it is approachable, then it isn't. But that is for spirits an ST must decide on the fly. What spirits do we know are part of Bunyip's brood, and what else should go there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-21T20:49:02-07:00","id":1440900,"title":"","content":"Australian Spirits Part 2<br /> <br /> In Rage Across Australia, we are given four spirits specifically mentioned as hostile to the Garou. Rainbow Serpent and Bunyip are listed as lost totems. Ghost Bunyip (a kind of Ancestor spirit, I suppose) are found in the Penumbra and represent the \u201cghosts\u201d of the Bunyip. Then there is Kurpanga, a kind of Wyld spirit which happens to be hostile now. I think we can safely include all of these in Bunyip's brood.<br /> <br /> So what else can we include? Before we do that, I think a discussion is needed about Australian spirits in general in the game, as we know some of these are friendly to Garou and should not be included,<br /> <br /> Australia separated from the other continents around 40 million years ago. And as a result of it being tectonically stable, it lacks events like earthquakes and volcano eruptions that recycle soil rock and soil. Due to erosion, its soil is generally poor which forced its flora and fauna to coexist. It lacked dominant species and encouraged great diversity. As a result, there are many unique species there. 95% Fungi, 85% Land Marsupials, 89% Land Reptiles, 94% Frogs, and 57% Mangroves are endemic there \u2013 they are not found anywhere else. Many of this wildlife, though not all, should be found among Bunyip and Rainbow Serpent.<br /> <br /> We know that many iconic Australian animals remain friendly to Garou, even serving as totems, so they can't be members of Bunyip's brood. Or they might have originally been, but decided to leave and join other broods after the destruction of the tribe. This is something STs can decide for themselves based on their own chronicle needs. We know Kangaroo, Owl, Kookaburra, Lyrebird, Possum, the Boobook Owl, Echidna, Crocodile, and Wedge-Tailed Eagle all act as totems for Garou. Some of these have obvious associations with existing brood. Kangaroo could be in Stag's brood as an obivious herbivore prey animal, but he would also be a good candidate as his own Incarna representing native wildlife that remains friendly or at least approachable to Garou. Given that I don't think it is possible to fit all remaining wildlife with the existing Major Incarna, I think it can be a good default. If someone has another suggestion as this Major Incarna, or even suggestions for additional broods of Australia, that would be awesome to hear. I have limited information concerning Australia, so any contributors from Australia are particularly welcomed.<br /> <br /> RAAus mentions that in the special conditions of the Australian Penumbra (due to \u201cspirit wards\u201d established by the Bunyip in the Penumbra that caused it to show not the spirit world of Australia currently, but as it was at the time of the Bunyip's original arrival), that the spirits of Pleistocene Megafauna, some of which qualify as Great Beasts, still exist in the Penumbra. So there are still spirits of giant kangaroos, diprotodons, huge wombats, marsupial lions, and even thylacines here! I would place all of these in Bunyip's brood, which makes Bunyip a special figure for Australian extinct animals similar to Griffin's role elsewhere. (This could be used to explain the Red Talon's interest in Australia \u2013 make contact with these spirits and extend Griffin's protection should Bunyip be failing in this since the lost of the tribe.)<br /> <br /> I think the relatively few marsupial carnivores should be part of Bunyip's brood. The Tasmanian Devil and Tiger Quoll slot in here easily. Most venomous and constricting snakes in Australia also fit, as these are connected to Rainbow Serpent. After that it becomes more difficult for me.<br /> <br /> Large flightless birds like emu and cassowary might fit here. They don't obviously belong to another spirit brood, and we need more spirits to make Australian Penumbra hostile to Garou. But I also understand why someone would place it in the more friendly native wildlife brood of Kangaroo.<br /> <br /> Hammer &amp; Klaive gave an example that the Bunyip used a specific bird spirit to create their boomerang fetishes, and since their destruction no Garou has been able to recreate that fetish. The books says bird spirits are still willing to enter other fetishes, so it's just some lore that was lost. But it might also be that some specific bird spirit was part of Bunyip's brood, and it specifically what was entered into that fetish and now it just refuses to cooperate.<br /> <br /> I would place other iconic Australian wildlife like bandicoots and wombats in other broods, perhaps Kangaroo, but existing broods might also make sense.<br /> <br /> Interestingly, the Dingo should not be part of Bunyip's brood. It arrived quite late to Australia, and despite Rage Across Australia allowing dingoes to be lupus breedstock, it explicitly mentions the Bunyip deliberately choose not to breed with dingoes, even though the arrival of dingoes coincided with the extirpation the thylacine from all of Australia except the island of Tasmania (and perhaps some isolated spots in SE Australia). So that meant the Bunyip accepted the destruction of the vast majority of their lupus members thousands of years before the arrival of Europeans. There can be many reasons for this, but it's a notable conclusion. If Dingo belongs anywhere, it best goes to Father Wolf's brood. At least there it would explain why lupus were-dingoes can exist in your chronicle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-21T20:51:08-07:00","id":1440901,"title":"","content":"Australian Spirits Part 3<br /> <br /> So we're looking at a small brood if we look only at appropriate wildlife, so this is probably a case where we need to look at thematic spirits to provide more variety, and utilize some of the other tools provided by Axis Mundi \u2013 spirits that volunteered to be under Bunyip or which Bunyip subjugated, and spirits created by Bunyip to serve a required function.<br /> <br /> Rainbow Serpent is associated with rains, storms, and floods. That intrudes on Grandfather Thunder's domain, but Australia is mainly a desert continent. It's the driest outside of Antartica. Scientifcally speaking, the lack of high mountains in western Australia (since Australia is tectonically stable, all of its mountains are therefore very old and thus heavily eroded) prevents the moisture laden ocean winds from being pushed into higher, colder altitudes where it condenses and falls as rain. There are mountains in southeastern Australia that allows some rain, but it also provides a rainshadow effect that prevents rain farther inland. So mythically, we might say that Rainbow Serpent, as ruler of the land, controls what moisture is available to Grandfather Thunder, and thus it and not Thunder determines when or whether it rains. So maybe there are spirit that are \u201crain providers\u201d that determine when Grandfather Thunder can summon rains.<br /> <br /> Some kind of warden or protective spirit of the \u201cwards\u201d in the penumbra that keeps the Dreamtime in stasis as it was 50,000 years ago. Around the time when mankind first appeared in Australia, Australia had higher monsoons and some sort of inland sea. But various factors created the climate we know today. So we could say the Bunyip were essential at \u201cfreezing\u201d the Penumbra as it used to be. This would be quite an epic achievement. So there should be guardian or caretaker spirits that helped maintain this kind of \u201cpenumbral zoo or nature preserve.\u201d There may also be some kind of time or memory spirit that perpetuates this memory. This might even explain why the Ghost Bunyip continue to exist in the Australian Penumbra.<br /> <br /> While there are various Australian wildlife that can be accommodated in existing broods, my tentative inclusion is that we need two additional broods to account for a lot of unique wildlife there \u2013 Bunyip and Kangaroo (or another Incarna instead, but so far I think Kangaroo fits best). Neither will get much play outside a chronicle set in Australia, but I think having these broods will add a lot to any Australian chronicles. The other prominent broods I think would include Raven, Turtle, Falcon, Rat, Owl, Shark, Dragon Kings, Cockroach, <br /> <br /> Uktena probably still has brood members there, but much of its normal role is otherwise usurped by Rainbow Serpent. Mainly some fish and some snakes that for whatever reason weren't part of Bunyip's brood. Stag would probably be absent, but most of its role as herbivores used as prey animals would be filled by Kangaroo instead. I don't see any real role that Fenris or Wendigo would naturally play, and their broods would be mostly absent (though there are cuckoo birds in Australia). The role of Pegasus is debateable. A sacred feminine spirit would seem to be universal, but the perculiarites of the Australian umbra assign a masculine moon spirit and feminine solar spirit. That doesn't mean Pegasus is blocked, but the way its brood manifests in Australia might be very confusing for the Black Furies. Unicorn and its brood is probably in a similar position as is Chimera \u2013 very minor and unimportant. Lastly there is Griffin, whose two main spheres of predators and extinct animals, would not find a welcome spot here. Australia lacked the kind of \u201cpredator kings\u201d Griffin is known for, and its role as protector of extinct spirits was effectively taken over by Bunyip.<br /> <br /> Needless to say, this would present a very confusing umbral landscape to Garou. Furthermore, as non-native species (rabbits, foxes, cane toads, feral goats, feral pigs, feral cats) arrive in Australia that Garou Incarna broods would protect, their often destructive role in the Australian ecosystem would provoke even more anger to the two most important spirit broods there. So we have a much better understanding of why this is such a hostile landscape for Garou. Their normal spirit allies can't help them, and if they summon them to do so, they may just make the dominant spirits even angrier. This fits very well with the themes of Rage Across Australia. It even explains why the Garou there most often choose totems \u201cnative\u201d to Australia instead of the more common totems as the sourcebook states. The Silver Fangs and Shadow Lords who continue to use traditional totems, are likely creating lots of problems for themselves.<br /> <br /> For roleplay purposes, I would say neither Bunyip or Kangaroo would speak the Garou tongue, though Bunyip would understand it, if in your game you keep them as Garou. Kangaroo's brood would be approachable and potentially an ally, but I don't think it would be overtly friendly. Individual relationships can be developed with chiminage, but attempting to win widespread friendship would be an epic task. Bunyip's brood, of course, is explicitly hostile and often harass or attack the Garou.<br /> <br /> I must say that for various reasons I had not thought much of the Rage Across Australia book except for some of the NPCs whom I liked. But in doing research for this spirit brood discussion, I am much more impressed. Things as written fit as they \u201cshould\u201d, though I doubt much thought was really given to the spirit brood concept. I think this is one location where utilizing a spirit brood approach really pays off a lot of dividends for STs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-23T22:20:30-07:00","id":1441194,"title":"","content":"Now we're getting to the more difficult conjectural broods. So I want to talk about the spirits of domesticated animals. These generally aren't important to Werewolf sourcebooks, and I suspect most chronicles never include them. But we do see there are a number of Garou totems that have to be the spirits of domesticated animals so we have a place to start.<br /> <br /> Canonical spirits include Cockerel (from Fianna tribebook), Father Mule (from Children of Gaia), Horse (Werewolf: the Dark ages), Bull, and Goat (both from Players Guide). <br /> <br /> Where should these go? We addressed one possible placement of Bull in the Large Herd Animals thread, so we could continue to do the same and place them in existing broods headed by a wildlife spirit or similar wild spirit. However, I think most domestic animal spirits likely belong to their own brood.<br /> <br /> A domestic animal brood would create roleplaying challenges for PCs, and distinguish them from other spirits. Dog spirits, for example, should act very differently than most animal spirits. <br /> <br /> Then there are issues of spirits like Horse and Bull who now only exist as domesticated breeds with wild breeds being extinct. I can imagine that when horses were wild, they were part of Pegasus's brood. That would be the default for a long time as horses were only domesticated around 3000 BC, and for a long time afterwards there were still very large numbers of wild horses. But by now, I think Horse would not fit well within Pegasus's brood as the Black Furies have always been depicted as one of the tribes most fiercely attached to the Wyld. I think it creates an interesting element if Horse ends up leaving Pegasus and joins another, friendlier brood. More opportunity for roleplay between spirits and Garou.<br /> <br /> The Horse totem in Werewolf: the Dark Ages is explicit about it being a spirit of a domesticated animal, and imposes penalties on its children on non-Warders in dealing with other Garou. And its ban involves never harming domesticated animals (I doubt this extends to not eating them). <br /> <br /> Mules, of course, are sterile and can't even propagate without mankind. The totem description mentions that hybrids are born in the wild, but I am ignorant of this happening with mules. And in extremely rare occasions mare mules have given birth, something even attested in Classical times, but these rare exceptions needn't concern us. The Father Mule totem likes metis best because both are sterile, and this is presumably the reason it is included with the Children of Gaia, as their tribe is most comfortable with metis. But I think Mule is best off in a domestic animal brood than under Unicorn. Since Mule is the child of Horse, I think it's best to follow the precedent here. I don't think Mule was intended to be a totem only available to a pack of Children of Gaia metis, but something available to all Metis of any tribe. And while the latter does not mean Mule couldn't be under Unicorn, I still think a separate brood would work best.<br /> <br /> The description of Cockerel or Rooster also explicitly mentions it being a spirit of a domesticated animal. It even mentions that many Garou disdain domestic animal totems, but that the Fianna have a more open mind. This cements the idea that there is a separate brood of such spirits, but that certain tribes still have affinities for one or more of its members. The ban here is that its children do not eat domesticated animals, which likely forces them to hunt. With the bans of both Horse and Cockerel, we are seeing a deep protective instinct among these totems.<br /> <br /> Unlike some other spirits here, Cockerel has lots of wild relatives, as junglefowl are still plenty in South Asia and Southeast Asia. These are part of the pheasant family which includes quail, turkey, patridges and many game birds. As an aside, I wonder what brood fits these wildfowl? As mainly terrestrial birds that do not fly much often hunted for game, I am leaning towards Stag. And that could explain the Fianna's interest in Cockerel.<br /> <br /> Bull is more difficult. Its description makes no mention of domestic animals, and it is supposed to be a popular totem. No mention of the normal scorn of the Garou for domesticated animals. I think this indicates Bull is not a member of that brood, and remains in its original brood of wild animals. As uncastrated male cattle, bulls are extremely aggressive and dangerous. This may explain why it remains outside. Although I imagine there is feminine counterpart, Cow, that would be part of a domesticated brood. This actually makes for an interesting dynamic, as Bull while aggressive and fierce, would still have some involvement with domesticated animal spirits. Perhaps it sees itself as more of man's partner than its servant, or look down upon the more docile animals but in a protective way.<br /> <br /> There's also Goat of the Player's Guide, which appears to be the domesticated goat, not wild goat, but makes no mentioned whatsoever of its status as a domesticated animal. However, it is also a Totem of Cunning. Perhaps that explains it. I don't think Goat would fit well under Bison or Stag as an alternative, but Trickster could fit. For now, I'm content to leave it here.<br /> <br /> While that exists the known totems, we know of the other spirits of domesticated animals because there's such a tiny list for them. Besides Dog, we also have goat, pig, sheep, cat, donkeys, domestic ducks and geese, guinea pigs, camels, silkmoths, llamas and alpacas, lab mice, certain fish and birds, and various pets. The most important of such species are mentioned earlier. So it should be a relatively small brood compared to others, but one whose members an ST could quickly determine whenever needed.<br /> <br /> I would not put these under Cockroach as cockroaches were never domesticated, and the spirit in Werewolf have very different affinities than domesticated animals. I like to vision Cockroach as a commensal animal spirit teaching the Glass Walkers how to exist alongside humanity, unseen. That's different than animals that serve man. I see many different individual personalities for each spirit \u2013 Dog being very loyal, Cat acting indifferent and more independent but liking the luxury of houses, Sheep being very submissive, Cow being very nurturing and protective of children, and so on.<br /> <br /> Who should be the Major Incarna of this brood? Dog, as man's best friend and longest domesticated animal, would be a natural choice. However, I wonder if the actual Major Incarna should be something like \u201cthe Master\u201d, not a spirit of humanity, but an aspect of humanity of man molding his environment. Something similar to the Patriarch spirit mentioned by the Black Furies. I think there are good and bad points to either option. My own leanings currently is to split the difference \u2013 refer to \u201cthe Master\u201d IC, but never ever show such a spirit. Instead, Dog is the de facto leader of the brood, but Dog makes many, many references to the Master. And all the other domesticated spirits don't question the existence of the Master, but there is never any proof provided.<br /> <br /> The domesticated animal spirits should still acknowledge Gaia, but they believe she has given the Master dominion over the whole world. They are probably ambivalent to Bone Gnawers and most friendly to the Glass Walkers, but likely feel increasingly rebuffed by that tribe for ignoring them (the traditional helpers of man) in favor of truly weird Weaver spirits. I think their reactions to the Glass Walkers vary by individual, but include a lot of warnings that the tribe is ignoring them too much now. The other Garou they typically dislike and think are dangerous (with various exceptions). And of course are enemies of Griffin and the Red Talons. Individual tribes could likely appeal to spirit of animals that once \u201cbelonged\u201d to their tribal incarna, or have some other affinity for the animal.<br /> <br /> I see these spirits as mainly located in rural areas, especially farms. As the landscape increasingly urbanizes, their variety and numbers drop off considerably until there is only Dog and Cat in the suburbs, and only infrequent appearances of these in the urban Penumbra which is hostile to most animal spirits. Here is where we would see a lot of feral animal spirits under Rat like Lost Dogs and Feral Psychagonea.<br /> <br /> I believe the best \u201chome\u201d for these spirits would be the Near Realm Wolfhome, which they would call by a different name. Wolfhome is so named because Garou are forced to assume lupus form while there and live simply as a wolf. But it has been inconsistently depicted. At times it is boundless forest. But it also has the Town which represents the dominion of Man, and Garou experience strange experiences there just like they were wolves captured by humans and subjected to things they simply can't understand. In my own chronicles, this is the Near Realm that shows the dominion of man over nature. A skilled ST would likely prevent the PCs from ever discovering that Wolfhome is the source of domesticated animal spirits. Still, if this bothers you, you can simply create a new Near Realm where these spirits originate from and go to if they reform.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-28T00:57:33-07:00","id":1441757,"title":"","content":"Trickster Part 1<br /> <br /> A lot of the prior posts were merely a delay before I finally had to deal with Trickster. This was one of the first broods I tried to work on, thinking it would be easy. But it brought up a lot of questions that I was forced to answer them by developing other broods before tackling this. But I've run out of obvious ones, so I'm now back to this.<br /> <br /> The main issues here are the nature of Coyote, and how to approach a brood with universal nature if its Major Incarna does not have its natural correspondence in most of the world. After all, for thousands of years no one in Asia, Africa, or Europe would think \u201ccoyote\u201d when they had a trickster figure. If they encountered a spirit of this brood, they'd have to assign it to some other figure.<br /> <br /> After a more detailed look at the Nuwisha Totems, I've changed my mind on some things. I really disliked the idea that we had Loki, Ptah, Kokpelli, and other human concepts mixed in an animal of North American origin that had none of that cultural baggage. It moved the totem away from Coyote. So I thought perhaps \u201cCoyote\u201d was actually a universal Trickster spirit who simply adopted the Coyote animal which was especially sacred to him, and he also manifested in these other ways. But that still left the problem that \u201cLoki\u201d couldn't actually be the \u201creal\u201d Loki as if that entity existed in any real way, it'd be as a spirit of the Astral Plane or High Umbra. Same thing with all the other totems. It also didn't make sense for the Nuwisa to use these terms. When would they have had the Loki totem \u2013 or any others \u2013 before they met the Europeans? I found it absurd that they would have such a totem before then. But if that is the case, what would the Nuwisha do for personal totems since all of them are based on human culture? So what to do?<br /> <br /> However, reading the totems more closely, I think it's less that these spirits are \u201cLoki\u201d or \u201cPan\u201d or \u201cChung Kuel\u201d. These are simply other names given by homids to certain aspects of Coyote. Instead, each of these spirits are a particular way in which Trickster manifests. What's important is identifying the role of each aspect. Then various names might be given to it.<ul><li>Ti Malice \u2013 Coyote as manipulator; the liar; perhaps the Mischief Maker.</li> <li>Loki \u2013 Coyote as warrior; the Monster-Slayer.</li> <li>Kishijoten \u2013 Coyote as nurturer or caregiver.</li> <li>Ptah \u2013 Coyote as creator.</li> <li>Kokopelli or Pan \u2013 Coyote as lover or pleasure seeker.</li> <li>Chung Kuel \u2013 Coyote as gambler or chance; perhaps also Coyote the Greedy</li> <li>Xochipilli \u2013 Coyote the Bold or Reckless (the fool without fear)</li> <li>Oghma \u2013 Coyote as wise teacher.</li> </ul>By substituting these universal archetypes for the culturally biased names, it solved all the problems I had. If you had the same issues, I hope it works for you too. If you had no problems with it, you can easily ignore it. In other words, rather than Trickster assuming the form of Coyote, it was Coyote who appropriated existing names.<br /> <br /> Of course the Nuwisha book also gave the Raven totem, which is clearly Raven and not an aspect of Coyote. However, we might saw that despite the name and identical description, this is merely Coyote as the riddler. That solves our problem. A bit lame that we'd now have two \u201cdifferent\u201d totems with the same power. So a better option would be to allow the Nuwisha to have personal totems that weren't an aspect of Coyote, but were spirits friendly to him. Raven is one, but so should Fox be. Which other Totems of Cunning are included would be up to the ST.<br /> <br /> Another issue is what to do about Fox. Fox would be the obvious choice for a trickster animal spirit in the Old World. One nice thing is that many Coyote legends involve Fox as a companion to Coyote \u2013 they're seen as buddies. If Coyote died in one of his adventures, it was Fox who would bring him back to life. So we do have some justification of including them in the same brood. Perhaps Fox is Coyote's deputy and in charge when or where he isn't around.<br /> <br /> The Kitsune doesn't fit the same role in the Old World as the Nuwisha do in the New World, but there are some similarities. One issue is that the game has an undecided approach to Kitsune. The Book of Caerns and Hengeyokai books take very different approaches to them, and the Kitsune breed doesn't match well with actual kitsune legends where they are depicted more like a fairy species. But the animal of fox clearly has trickster qualities in any human culture, so ultimately I am ruling any issues that need to be fixed probably belong to the Kitsune themselves rather than the spirit.<br /> <br /> Another possibility is that the brood is lead not by one Incarna, but a group of them \u2013 a kind of \u201cfriends of Coyote\u201d where Coyote, Fox, and possibly other animal trickster archetypes act as common protectors of the brood, but not one is clearly dominant. That's anomalous among spirits, but isn't necessarily out of place for tricksters. I personally would not include Raven among this group as I think it's association with Helios and the dead makes Raven sufficiently different. But different STs may have other tastes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-28T00:59:40-07:00","id":1441758,"title":"","content":"Trickster Part 2<br /> <br /> So I'm now willing to go with one brood for the trickster, and that Coyote is the Major Incarna of it. I don't think there's many spirits that would fit in this category, and splitting them among two or more broods makes even less sense. I would limit the brood mostly to spirits that are clearly trickster type spirits, often inspired by real folklore, and leave out others \u2013 including some of the other Totems of Cunning that don't quite qualify for the trickster archetype. But who?<br /> <br /> The Rabbit totem of the revised Wendigo tribebook works. I put Hare in Raven, but rabbits are often trickster heroes in many stories so Coyote should have one too.<br /> <br /> Roadrunner is listed as a totem of cunning in Uktena revised. But outside of Warner Bros. cartoons, I am not sure how trickstery that animal is considered. Wikipedia simply says Indians consider the bird to be a protector against evil, so it may be best to leave him with Uktena.<br /> <br /> As mentioned in the previous post on spirit of domesticated animals, Goat could fit here.<br /> <br /> I would like to place Raccoon here, but Axis Mundi assigns him to Rat. But an ST could decide to change that, or allow \u201cRaccoon in the city\u201d be part of Rat, but allow Raccoon in the wilderness be here.<br /> <br /> Likewise Carcajou/Wolverine of Innu myth is a trickster and mentioned in Axis Mundi under Wendigo We could put the normal &quot;Wolverine&quot; under Wendigo, and move &quot;Carcajou&quot; to Coyote. That would reduce the diversity of Wendigo's brood a bit, but might be worth it.<br /> <br /> Otter is often a trickster animal. Earlier I placed him possibly in Turtle, but he could fit here. And like many animal spirits, we could place different spirits of the same animal in different broods to emphasize different correspondences of that spirit. Like many of these trickster animal spirits, its version (Kiunik) is listed among the spirits of Wendigo. I think this is less likely a deliberate attempt to introduce a trickster element to Wendigo (I don't recall anything like that explicitly mentioned in tribebooks or other tribal descriptions), than someone pillaging a book of Native American myths and dumping them into the \u201cIndian tribe.\u201d However, it could be an interesting element of Wendigo. Perhaps Coyote instructed some of his brood to join Wendigo in order that he and his children had something that could lighten their mood and fill their cold hearts with laughter. Perhaps we could build on this and explain that Wendigo and Coyote actually get along well.<br /> <br /> Minks and muskrats are also considered to be trickster style animals in many Indian legends. Blue Jays are too, but as they are corvids, they best fit under Raven. That might be the same for the Whiskey Jack totem also listed in the Wendigo tribebook. <br /> <br /> We know that Raven is particularly close to the Wendigo like he is to the Get of Fenris. So maybe both Coyote and Raven are very close to Wendigo. Earlier I mentioned that Wendigo was a brood I found very hard to expand. Maybe the solution to provide Wendigo Garou more spirit allies is not to expand Wendigo's brood. But to acknowledge the tribe is also particularly close to the brood of Coyote and Raven. The ancient Wendigo might have determined that Wendigo's brood, while potent, was also limited. And in response forged very close ties to the broods of Coyote and Raven. The tribe could be said to already have strong affinity with these broods, and the spirits might treat the tribe almost as if they were their children. That would serve the purpose of providing the Wendigo with more spirit allies, while still keeping its brood appropriate, and add some strong roleplay elements to these spirit broods. I will need to ponder this idea a lot more, but my initial thoughts are that I like it and think it has a lot of potential. But let's get back to the topic.<br /> <br /> Monkeys are often considered to be silly or foolish animals, and they would fit among coyote. Maybe not all monkeys, but a selection of the most \u201cfoolish\u201d among them. These might include the capuchin monkeys, but there are other candidates among New World and Old World monkeys. We might even want to say that Monkey King totem, mentioned in Glass Walkers tribebook, is part of Coyote's brood. Maybe even one of \u201cCoyote's friends\u201d.<br /> <br /> It'd be nice to have some sort of spider figure given the tales of Ananse (or Iktomi of the Lakota Sioux), but I don't think that's an existing totem or spirit that qualifies. Something for the ST to create themselves. Give Ananse's importance as trickster hero in much of Africa, this spirit should be around the equal of Coyote and Fox. But the game has a were-spider breed with its own game legend around Spider, so it's a neglected aspect.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-03-28T01:02:17-07:00","id":1441759,"title":"","content":"Trickster Part 3<br /> <br /> Trickster has many angles \u2013 clown, malevolence, discord, problem solver, bringer of fire, teacher, and many more. In terms of Coyote's role among Garou and the spirit world in general, I can see a variety of spirits that could be based on these correspondences \u2013 created by Coyote to fill one of his purposes. We just need to think of what kind of spirit could be a helper in one of his totem aspects.<br /> <br /> The totem spirit Momentum, mentioned in Book of the City, could easily be one such spirit, especially for Chung Kuel/ Coyote the Gambler. There could also be spirits of mirth similar to the Bragir spirits of Fenris. I think an ST can think up a large number of appropriate spirits that could serve a need to one of Coyote's aspects.<br /> <br /> I originally felt it would be difficult to round out a brood for Trickster/Coyote. But after going through this thought process, I am surprised at how many spirits I can easily fit, and see open spots where I can probably create more spirits if needed to fill them. By combining Coyote with his powerful deputies of Fox and others, I think this is a very strong brood with lots of different spirits that most tribes would find very useful. I also liked the inadvertent connection to Wendigo (along with Raven) which supplements Wendigo's brood nicely and offers lots of good roleplay opportunities.<br /> <br /> The first edition Players Guide has a story that explains the Nuwisha once had auspices like the Garou, but Luna cursed them to be only Ragabash after Coyote played a trick on her. I like that tale a lot, and use it as justification that one time the Nuwisha \u201cwere\u201d a tribe of Garou just like the jackals of the Silent Striders were (during the \u201ctime before time\u201d mythic sense), but as a result are now accounted as one of the Fera. So Nuwisha may have allies among the other Garou totems still, just like they were any other tribe. Given the Nuwisha's reputation as umbral travelers, such an interpretation could support that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-07T00:40:13-07:00","id":1442939,"title":"","content":"I think that may have been the last of the spirit broods that I develop to any degree. A summary post will come up at some point. But this certainly isn't the limit of possible spirit broods. After all, there are lots of other wildlife and functions not covered. But just like the various ocean Major Incarna are probably insufficient to cover the fullness or marine life but it's irrelevant given the nature of the game and most chronicles, so are these other types. But let's give some brief thought to them.<br /> <br /> An obvious gap are terrestrial arthropods \u2013 wildlife of insect, arachnid, and myriapod origin. Cockroach is an obvious totem with a spirit brood among them, and we have other Garou pack totems detailed in sourcebooks like Tsetse Fly, Scarab, Dragonfly, and Butterfly. But overall these aren't typical spirits encountered in the game, and while some should fall in broods lead by more traditional animal spirits (or others), I think it's obvious more would fall in broods lead by spirits of arthropods. So let's do a little creative brainstorming.<br /> <br /> Obviously Spider should be one. The Ananasa breed book should be mentioned here. It introduces a lot of new concepts, most of which I do not like. The most important being that the breed's creator/ruler is NOT Spider, but Queen Ananasa who was created directly by the Weaver and is kind of an ersatz ruler/creator of all the various arthropods (insects as well as spiders, though spiders are her favorite). And there's a lot of other dubious things like introducing the Insect Wars and how the Ananasi (which really can't be Ananasi since this happens long before mammals or mankind is even around) killed the equivalent insect breeds. I have massive problems with this which would require its own post or even thread. But in the end, the result is that there is absolutely nothing we need to take from the breedbook into this discussion since there's nothing in it that is useful except that Queen Ananasi is not Spider (though it is mentioned Queen Ananasa mated with Spider in order to create the Ananasi). In fact, the breedbook is explicit that the Ananasi do not worship any other totem spirits \u2013 that is strictly forbidden \u2013 their equivalent Litany mandates that only Queen Ananasa should be worshipped. That would exclude the Spider spirit as well!<br /> <br /> But <b>Spider</b> should still be important even if it is never mentioned as a Garou totem. But if you go by the breedbook, it is likely not as powerful as it should be given that the Ananasi don't actually make any devotions to it.<br /> <br /> Spider might just include spiders, but it might include many other arachnids as well including scorpions and ticks. Or if you want to keep the weaving web theme for Spider, then maybe dump most other arachnids into its own brood maybe under <b>Scorpion</b>. Scorpions are important because they are often associated with treachery and malevolence. Aesop's Fable of the Scorpion and the Frog is well known. I like to roleplay scorpion spirits as Gaian, but inherently treacherous. They'll always turn on you regardless of their agreements because that is their nature. Tick is interesting just because of its role in the W20 SAS of the Skinner when it appears as a spirit hoping to get its own Garou tribe and propel it to Incarna status. Is it an independent spirit, or currently under some other spirit's brood? It's plot in the Skinner does lend it a somewhat treacherous nature that would work well under Scorpion.<br /> <br /> I found it interesting that Tick was only a Jaggling spirit, and not an Incarna in its own right as leader of its entire species. I'm not sure that I like that as Jagglings are potentially easy to destroy, which means alltick species could be conceivably wiped out if that Tick jaggling is destroyed. I'd probably just make Tick in that scenario an important Jaggling avatar sent by the Tick Incarna to do its bidding in the rest of the Umbra. It's just a very lesser Incarna, in the same class as other Incarna, but having little power compared to others. Since Incarna are not supposed to be approachable anyway, perhaps that was even the intention.<br /> <br /> We should probably have some sort of spirit for the most weavery of the insects \u2013 ants and bees and other eusocial insects. That would include Termite which is mentioned as a Garou totem in the Book of the City. And if Termite is a totem, then Bee and Ant should be as well. I can't see Termite as having more spiritual power than them. Personally I think <b>Ant</b> would be the superior choice as Major Incarna. One thing I did not know until now is that ants, bees, and wasps are actually part of the same order \u2013 hymnoptera. Although Termites aren't, it's a good basis for the brood. The creatures here have very distinctive cultural qualities being associated with strong work ethic, social cohesion, and extensive colonies.<br /> <br /> What else? I think <b>Fly</b> would make a good choice. Flies are a heavily symbolic animal \u2013 usually negative, but not always. Again, we have Tsetse Fly as a pre-existing totem, this time mentioned in the Silent Striders tribebook. Although not a pack totem, we know Mosquitoes are part of Wendigo's brood (there are lots of moquitos on the tundra). All of these are under the order Diptera. In my own chronicles, I use Fly as an Incarna corrupted by the Wyrm, and I think this fits well. If Fly is now allied to the Wyrm, then its Gaian counterparts need to go to other broods. Tundra based mosquitoes could go to Wendigo, and other spirits might go to Dragon Kings, Cockroach, Turtle, or more unusual broods. The need for certain fly spirits to find safe homes could even lead to strange alliances that otherwise might now make sense, like sticking Tsetse Fly under Owl if you want to go that route. Of course, if you want Fly to be Gaian, that works too. I just like having some other corrupted spirits out there besides Bat. Flies are single wing insects, so Diptera doesn't include a lot of insects with \u201cfly\u201d in its name like Dragonfly.<br /> <br /> There are more types of Beetles in the world than any other. 25% of all animal life forms are some form of beetle! And Scarab \u2013 an exotic name for the dung beetle \u2013 is a pack totem in the Silent Striders tribebook. Maybe we want <b>Scarab</b> explicitly as leader of that brood. Beetles don't usually have negative connotations (though some are pests) and lack the disgust reaction many insects have. In fact another well known beetle \u2013 the ladybug \u2013 has very positive connotations. A lot of insects seen as beneficial or friendly could go here.<br /> <br /> These are very obvious associations. Ant, Fly, and Scarab have very obvious correspondences, and they all have obvious hooks for roleplaying them. But they likely don't include all other insects. What other ones might be distinctive enough to be their own thing? In my opinion, other distinctive insects include Mantis and Grasshopper. They not only look different, but also have distinct cultural associations \u2013 the grasshopper species with making sound (music) and the mantis as an animal that prays, devours baneful insects, and also known for its cannibalistic mating. Though I won't spend further time developing these broods, I think they have potential Major Incarna for those STs that need additional insect spirit broods.<br /> <br /> I think there are many more potential broods out there, but the five already identified should be more than sufficient for any needs in game. Spider, Ant, Fly, and Scarab (and maybe Scorpion) cover a lot of ground, and I think it'll be the rare chronicle that needs more than that. But if you need more go ahead! I'd love to hear why and what you picked.<br /> <br /> Whether these broods include non-insect animals is up to the ST, but I would likely rule them not. That makes insect based broods fairly limited, but I think that is OK. In the vast majority of chronicles, these spirits won't play any role, much less an important one. But if any come to prominence, the ST can quickly assign them to their own brood, and roleplay them as being distant and alien to the Garou, though still approachable. And as for Fly, any disreputable corrupt insect spirit could be shown there.<br /> <br /> Another thing you might want to do is keep these spirits and their broods in their own little micro-world that rarely intrude on other spirits. Make them very alien and difficult to communicate with except for those few that have explicit alliances with the Garou (pack totems), making them something as odd ducks among their broodmates.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-10T01:28:55-07:00","id":1443348,"title":"","content":"Plant Spirits 1<br /> <br /> Next comes one of the other great kingdoms \u2013 plants. Plants as spirits are treated very differently than others. Animal spirits and animals are separate things, but plant spirits reside in plants and can be awakened. We're even told that these spirits do awaken independently on the first day of spring and that they slumber in winter. The first Wendigo tribebook says some plants are &quot;natural fetishes&quot; that any Garou can use. And certain plant spirits seem to be fairly important in the Umbra and can even be Garou pack totems. Some such spirits are part of existing broods. Let's start with those.<br /> <br /> Grain is part of Stag's brood, although she is primarily a spirit of grain alcohol. She does have some sway over actual grain fields. That fits in a \u201cStag represents the bounty of the land\u201d aspect. Axis Mundi also gives us \u201cCorn\u201d, but this would be the spirit better known as Grain or perhaps Cereal as it represents all kind of domesticated plants of the harvest \u2013 wheat, rye, rice, maize, etc. (this appears to be an indepdent spirit) And the Croatan had Corn Maiden which was the explicit spirit of maize. Although she can be a pack totem, and was probably popular with the Croatan, the totem description mentions she is explicitly affiliated with the Weaver. That means she is probably not part of Turtle's brood. Perhaps all domesticated crops are part of an independent brood that has the taint of the Weaver on them, perhaps lead by \u201cCorn\u201d/Cereal. This brood offers some good roleplay opportunity. They aren't hostile to the Garou, although except for Corn Maiden might not be especially friendly. But they're a very useful group of plants for obvious reasons as they feed humanity.<br /> <br /> Axis Mundi gives us Psychedelic Plant Totems who have &quot;powerful enigmatic spirits&quot; that reveal visions. Tobacco, Ayahuasca, Peyote, and (some) Mushrooms are mentioned, although only Ayahuasca and Peyote are listed as possible pack totems and spirit descriptions. We're told consuming such plants are a sacrament, but that the rite of spirit awakening must be performed on them first. I think these are very powerful spirits and separate from usual broods. Whether each one is independent, or they belong to a single brood is probably best left decided by individual STs. I'm not sure what common Incarna they might have unless it is something very nebulous. Given they are supposed to be enigmatic, it may be best to leave these a mystery. Although this may have some relevance to the Dark Fungus pack totem of the Black Spiral Dancers. Likely it is a corrupt version of the same \u201cclass\u201d of spirits.<br /> <br /> The Oak tree spirit is especially important. Oak has an ancient alliance with Stag made when the world was young. While it's said that Oak and Stag made alliance with the Fianna. Therefore Oak sometimes extends his help to Fianna. We're also told that they won't break alliances with Packs of Stag. I like the idea that Oak has its own pact with the Fianna and Stag. Players and STs can do a lot with this.<br /> <br /> We're also told that Oak and all other tree spirits serve the Incarna of the World Tree (also known by various names like Tree of Life, Cosmic Tree, etc.) Maybe it is World Tree that is the Major Incarna of trees and all trees are part of its brood. But the World Tree has lots of correspondences with it. Its subservant spirit don't need to embody all of them, but there's going to be a lot of additional spirits in that brood.<br /> <br /> If not, then perhaps Oak is the incarna of all deciduous trees, and a spirit like Spruce or Pine is the incarna ruling over all evergreen spirits. I like the distinction between evergreen and deciduous plants, but perhaps it's best to not make them separate broods. Just roleplay them differently. What do people think?<br /> <br /> So we have World Tree and probably a spirit I will call \u201cCereal\u201d for the domesticated harvest crops. What else makes sense? Plants are incredibly diverse, so I'm not so concerned with trying to organize the entire Plant Kingdom into a brood just as larger or larger than we've done for Animals. That isn't needed. We just have to concentrate on the most likely plant spirits to be encountered in game, and be able to help them. That limits our needs to obvious plant spirits encountered in game (like Glade Children) and those plants that could be used through the Rite of Spirit Awakening or perhaps as \u201cnatural fetishes\u201d by PCs.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-10T01:30:27-07:00","id":1443349,"title":"","content":"Plant Spirits 2<br /> <br /> This is even more subjective than usual, but I would want to divide plants (or Incarna) into the following groups \u2013 Trees, Crops, Grasses, Cacti, Shrubs, Herbs, Nightshades, Fungi, and Poisons. I think that should cover most of what we need. Explanations are below.<br /> <br /> Trees and Crops are already covered.<br /> <br /> Grasslands are a very important ecosystem, and that lends itself to some kind of Wild Grass to be its own Incarna. They cover around a third of all land areas and support the greatest number of wildlife on Earth. Since \u201cWorld Tree\u201d is very generic, I think the equally generic \u201cWild Grass\u201d is fine too. And if Oak/Tree is corrected to Stag, then maybe Wild Grass is connected in some way to the Bull/Bison/Elephant lead brood of large herbivores who are best known as grass eaters.<br /> <br /> Cacti are very distinctive. Except for one species, all are native to the Americas. They probably deserve their own brood lead by Saguaro. I don't think they get much coverage though Aloe should have been listed as one of the plant examples for the rite of spirit awakening in my opinion. Peyote is a cactus, but per above should be in its own special category.<br /> <br /> Shrubs are just woody plants that don't get to be trees, and some species can either become shrubs or trees depending on how much water they get. Other than I think they should be covered separately, I don't have many ideas on what to do with them. This is really just a default category to dump stuff into. However, the Hawthorn plant is usually a shrub (or small tree) and has lots of folklore attributes to it. Among other things hawthorm is said to make entrance to the fairy otherworld, and said to be useful against vampires. So we have something potent. Both hawthorns and roses are part of the Rosacae family, and roses can be shrubs too.<br /> <br /> Herbs are another generic category of many herbaceous plants that often have medicinal value and many folkloric attributes. Many plants that are useful talens by the rite of spirit awakening are based here. Many of these are in the mint/sage family. So we might want to use Mint.<br /> <br /> Nightshades cover a wide variety of plants that are both poisonous and used as food. They are the Solanacae family and include tomatoes, potatoes, eggplants, and many kinds of peppers. I would keep only wild species here; domesticated versions should go with Cereal. Tobacco is found here too, but since it is considered so spiritually potent, I would place it with the other spiritually powerful and enigmatic spirits like Peyote and Ayahuasca. And of course there is belladonna, or deadly nightshade. Unlike other members of its family, I don't think any cultivars ever became edible by humans. But the plant is very well known in folklore as both a potent poison and as an essential ingredient in magic. Most recipes of the flying ointments of witchcraft include belladonna, and it's believed it is the hallucigenic ingredient that caused people to believe they were flying. It's also known as having many medicinal properties. I would put Belladonna as its Incarna.<br /> <br /> Science has now demonstrated that fungi aren't plants, and in fact are more closely related to animals. So perhaps its wrong to put them in the plant category technically, but why discuss them separately? They come in all sorts of varieties \u2013 edible, medicinal, poisonous, and many others. Clearly they should be represented. Just to keep things generic, like with World Tree and Wild Grass, I may just call its Incarna to be Toadstool.<br /> <br /> Lastly are the Poisons which cover all those plants that are considered poisonous to man or animals. We can also put skin irritating plants like poison ivy and poison oak here. I wanted this as a separate category for one simple reason \u2013 I wanted Wolfsbane, also known as aconite, as a Major Incarna in charge of its own brood. Werewolf: the Apocalypse has been strangely silent covering this iconic plant with much folkloric association with werewolfery. By cementing its status as a Major Incarna, I think we can promote it to having a stronger use in game.<br /> <br /> I don't think plant Incarna and their broods will get much use in chronicles, but some people might find some sort of schema useful when running games. If you have players who like to make talens using the Rite of Spirit Awakening, or want to make plant spirits more important by distinguishing between certain biomes or landscapes versus other types, this could help you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-13T21:01:52-07:00","id":1443832,"title":"","content":"That may be it for me. I covered a lot of ground here, and provided a lot more detail and depth than intended. It's probably too much for most STs (I doubt I'll ever use some of the broods I've explored here), but there could be others out there who could use even more broods!<br /> <br /> But for now the question is did I miss anything obvious? Is there any wildlife which is clearly not covered? Or any new function in the spirit world that should be represented by its own brood, or assigned to an existing brood?<br /> <br /> There are other Incarna spirits mentioned in the sourcebooks, but they aren't ones I think need much development. Charyss is the Incarna that rules the Near Realm of Erebus. The Black Furies tribebook mentions the Patriarch Incarna (said to be a servant of Abhorra, the Urge Wyrm of Hate, so this is more likely a spirit like Misogynist and would have a counterpart like Misandrist, as well as every other kind of hatemongering spirits). Bone Gnawers mentions the Great Trash Heap. The description of Heap gafflings of Rat's brood in Axis Mundi implies that most of these spirits are part of an independent brood of the Great Trash Heap. Insofar as these oddball Incarna might have broods, they are probably limited to immediate servitors. I'm sure there lots of known Incarna I missed, but I think most would fall in this category.<br /> <br /> Any way, now is the time to add your comments, suggestions, and ideas before I do a summary post. I don't think I have any more ideas on this in me, but who knows what might spark an idea. I'm very interested in any contributions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}